{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '45,98p' docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md\n sed -n '2300,2460p' docs/planning/decisions.md\n cat docs/planning/p09-recut.md\"","aggregated_output":"| P32 | First-run import returns to the wizard | A v1 import during first run commits its tenants through the reviewed C23 flow; the desktop then has a tenant, so the first-run draft ends (its credentials are cleaned up) and the shell opens on the imported tenant; its scan key is added in Administration (the reviewed C08/C09 credential setup) | A tenant that exists is never sent back through a first-run wizard (P30), and the wizard cannot wrap an existing tenant without duplicating it |\n| P33 | `ReadStatus` as its own core scope with per-product states from dedicated reads | `readTenant` admits one finite read per chosen product through the existing management pipeline: the Runtime overview the shell already owns (sessions chart, key inventory, the chosen Gateway workspace's usage and limits), the Red Team statistics and scan list (C13's read), the model scans, skill statistics and skill scans (C13's read); each settles into the tenant's read record as a per-source outcome (read, needs a role, failed); a source counts as read only when it established what it is for: a listing whose page is the inventory (not capped, nothing beyond the first page), a sessions chart with consistent totals, a key page with every expiration reported, Gateway usage with the day's requests and errors both known; anything less is named in `sourcesMissing` (the same gaps the Inbox shows). A product attempt that established nothing (every source failed, or the read never reached the service) is kept as the latest failed attempt beside the product's last outcome that did, which stays shown as older; the read is failed while such an attempt is the latest (a missing role is a settled attempt reported on the product, not a failed read). Once anything was attempted, every chosen source nothing has reached yet is named as unread coverage too, and `settledAt` carries the time of the latest attempt so a completion that established nothing is announced for when it settled, never as a first read still pending. A healthy empty read older than the ten-minute window (`OverviewView.fresh`, C12) is a gap: Home and the Inbox keep the cached read and name its age instead of an all-clear, never re-reading on their own; a refresh in progress keeps that read and its age named until the new read lands (admission restores nothing). A chosen Gateway with no workspace is unread coverage, named in `sourcesMissing`; `recheckProduct` re-reads one chosen product. The read completes (one generation) when every part admitted under the tenant's current context has landed; a read in which no source answered is a failed read (`failed`, with `failedAt`), never pending and never done, and a failed refresh keeps the earlier read labelled as older | No second read pipeline, and Home never claims coverage a read did not establish (I4): revised after the C30 attempt-01 review, which found selected Red Team and model/skill products left unread and a workspace-less Gateway announced as complete |\n| P34 | Inbox items with `kind`, `severity` and `explanation` per item | `InboxEntry` carries the overview item (Runtime, Gateway) or the C29 setup warning with its hold (listed, with every action, whatever the read status: a warning exists before any read); the explanation is per kind in the presentation's copy, not invented per item; `deadline` is absent (P20). Its action carries an `InboxTarget` (destination, product, Administration inventory, item name): a key item opens Administration on the API keys inventory filtered to the key, a setup warning opens where it is fixed (Settings for the scan key or management client, AI Gateway, AI Red Teaming) | The admitted reads report no per-item explanation or deadline; the target was added after the attempt-01 review found actions landing on the destination's default view |\n| P35 | Guard offers \"Archive and switch\" / \"Discard and switch\" | Archive ends the conversation (its protected history stays as C20 saved it); Discard ends it, removes its protected history record (after any save under way, under the same lock the sink holds) and refuses every later save of it (the turn the stop cuts short), then drops it once its task returns; both then switch once; the pending approval is never decided for the operator; the dialog's initial focus is Stay, the safe default (Enter on arrival ends nothing). A save admitted before the discard (the turn the engine's end cut short) re-checks the tombstone under the history hold before it writes, so it can never write the discarded transcript back after the removal. A removal that fails (storage unavailable, access denied) is never implied to have succeeded: the record stays listed and the workspace's history reports the failure, through unrelated opens and removals, until the operator removes the record | The engine is stopped either way: a switch retires its binding (C20); the difference is whether the transcript is kept, which the dialog promises and the attempt-01 review found unkept |\n| P36 | Completing \"Add a tenant\" switches to the new tenant | `completeTenantSetup{resolution?}` goes through the same guard as `selectTenant`: a pending Harness decision refuses it with `switchBlocked{to: <draft id>, completion: true}`, nothing is committed or retired, the draft stays, and the wizard renders the one guard dialog and re-sends the completion with the resolution | Wizard completion is listed among the entry points of the single guarded switch path (shell.md, I1); the attempt-01 review found it bypassing the guard |\n| P37 | Scopes are per tenant and a switch round trip shows the cached state | Each opened tenant owns its read record in the core (its last overview, the other products' outcomes, generation, recheck, snooze receipt) for the session; a switch away and back shows it again without a new read. Per-operation bookkeeping is separate from the cached data: each read part is tracked with the context it was admitted under, \"reading\" means a part under the current context is open, a part under a retired context is withdrawn when it lands (never recorded, never counted), so a read held open across a switch round trip can never leave the tenant reading. The presentation's own transient state follows the tenant too: the shell store carries the shown scope (set from each snapshot before it renders); a toast for a tenant's item or read and an unconfirmed delivery bound to a context are refused at insertion when that scope is no longer shown (a refusal or lost delivery arriving after a switch), retired on leaving it (however long Reduce Motion would keep them) and filtered at render; a contextual arrival belongs to the tenant it was followed in | I2 names the tenant, not the context, as the owner of its cached scopes; the attempt-01 review found the overview bound to the context and lost across A → B → A |\n| P38 | Snooze toast states the core-computed return time | The core issues a `SnoozeReceipt{id, title, returnsAt, sequence}` on the tenant's Inbox scope for the last snooze placed in the session; the shell announces a new sequence once, in the tenant it was placed in, and never a hold a switch or a reread merely rediscovers; a snooze ends at its return time whether or not its entry is actionable, and the core's deadline ticker publishes the expiry (the holds in effect are part of the overview's time signature) | I11 wants one source for the return time and one announcement per snooze; diffing holds between snapshots announced old holds after a switch (attempt-01 review) |\n| P39 | `AdminInventory` generated from screens/administration-inventory.md | Implemented as a Rust table (`admin_inventory::inventory`) carried on `ShellSnapshot.administration` in the v2 shell only (null in the legacy shell, whose literal snapshot and leak canary are unchanged; the presentation's fixture copy is pinned to the table by a core test); a unit test and the `ui_v2_reviews` suite pin every offered (kind, action) to `changes::is_allowed` and every denied pair to its refusal, so a label can never outrun the core. Read and Change tabs render the retained views (the same reads and reviewed changes) embedded under the accordion with the inventory's labels; Unavailable tabs and products show their gate and nothing else; Removed tabs are not rendered. The Gateway MCP servers tab lists the chosen workspace's catalog through `listMcpServers` | One source of truth for dispositions, checked against the matrices the core enforces (V2-T20, V2-R11) |\n| P40 | `openReview`/`confirmReview{typed}` intents | The retained review intents are the review (`reviewChange` binds tenant, context, credentials and the read baseline; `requestApply{confirmation}` validates the typed name in the core and opens the native confirmation, whose private completion alone applies). The v2 sheet disables Apply until the typed text equals the review's name and renders the applied panel only from the core's result and readback | The C09 review already carries every binding I3 asks for; new intents would duplicate it |\n| P41 | Rotation state per tenant with two stored timestamps | `Tenant.rotations` (persisted, never a secret): driven from settled `ApiKeys` results of the active context (a Version read back starts one; an unknown Version starts an unconfirmed one; a failed one records nothing; the old key's Delete read back absent completes it; a rejected Delete keeps it with the reason; an unknown one is reconciled by a complete key inventory read), plus `acknowledgeDeployment{application}` stored once. The reminder is the Inbox item `rotation-<application>` while a key is confirmed and the old one not read back gone. The one-time secret stays on its change result for the context it was applied in: Reveal is offered only while the core holds it; any sheet exit forgets it and a context change drops it | The issued secret must never be persisted (I6) while the stage and timestamps must survive reads, switches and relaunches (I7) |\n| P42 | Playground topic examples classify under the topic | The scan result carries `topics{violation, blocked, allowed}` from the service's `topic_violation` flag and `topic_guardrails_details`, shown apart from the detector flags: a matched topic is \"flagged (blocked)\" or \"flagged (monitor only)\", no match under the profile is \"Not detected\", an unreported verdict says so; detectors stay listed beside it. The prototype's local classifier is not carried: verdicts come from the service | The desktop never invents a verdict (DESIGN-V2 §10 gap 2 closed with service data) |\n| P43 | C31 live row runs through `xtask live-run` | `live-run --family L03 --subject C31` runs the C31 topic routine inside L03's designation and writes its receipts and run record under evidence/C31/live, leaving C25's receipts untouched (the consumption ledger stays C25's single ledger) | The live row needs the reviewed runner and the owner's designation, not a second harness |\n| P44 | Lifecycle conversations are seeded data in the prototype (`CONVOS`) | The three lifecycles are fixtures in the contracts package (`fixtures/wire/harness-lifecycle-{blocks,mcp,keys}.json`), one `ConversationView` per step (asking, approved, declined, ended) plus the saved transcript. Each read is one of the desktop's own read tools (`desktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`): blocks `search`, mcp `mcpServers`, keys `inventory` of API keys. They are regression inputs and boards only: a real tenant never shows them (I12) | The core decides approvals; fixtures must be shapes the core emits, validated by `parseShellSnapshot` |\n| P45 | The mcp and keys follow-ups open a guardrail review and the key rotation flow | `proposeChange` admits, besides the Runtime and DLP kinds, an API key `version` (the hand-off of a rotation: it opens the C31 review sheet and its staged sequence) and a guardrail of the conversation's own Gateway workspace (create or update only), staged with that workspace's reference so its review is bound to it; Gateway deletes and every other Gateway change, and a key rotation in place, stay refused. The follow-up of a key hand-off shows the rotation stage from `ShellSnapshot.rotations`. The mcp lifecycle's guardrail is described as what it is (a pii-check guardrail on all messages while the unlisted server is reviewed); no guardrail expresses an MCP allow list | A proposal only ever opens the operator's own review (D014); the follow-ups must be core-owned, not page-invented |\n| P46 | `endConversation` returns an archive id | `endHarness` keeps its receipt; the ended conversation names its saved transcript (`ConversationView.historyId`, the protected history record, shown only with save evidence: `savedAt` set and no `saveFailure`) and the rail marks it ended; \"Open the saved transcript\" opens that record | The archive already exists as the C20 protected history; a second id would duplicate it |\n| P47 | The mcp lifecycle reads `GatewayRead::McpServers` (P16) | A new desktop read tool `mcpServers` reads the conversation workspace's MCP catalog (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, the read the MCP panel uses) for the engine, decided in the page like every desktop read | The prototype's AES inventory has no admitted service; the Gateway catalog is the admitted read P16 named |\n| P48 | Read-only tool calls are approved in the page (C21 assumed) | Every desktop read tool (inventory, detail, session, search, Red Teaming, model observation, scan, test cases, MCP catalog) now waits for the operator's decision in the page (`ApprovalKind::DesktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`); declined, nothing is read and the engine is told so. Staging a proposal reads nothing and still runs at once. Before C32 these reads ran without asking | The lifecycles' approve and decline paths, and the operator's control over what tenant data reaches the model |\n| P49 | The core decides from the tool's write flag whether the native confirmation is required (C21 assumed) | No such branch existed: every decision was in-page. The core now classifies each decision (`HarnessApproval.effect`): a desktop read, or a command while the project is read-only, is `readOnly`; a file change, a command with changes allowed and any workspace MCP tool (whose effect the desktop cannot know; tools carry no trusted annotations) is `mayChange`, and allowing it opens the native confirmation (`Effect::ConfirmHarness`), whose private completion alone sends the allow; refused, the decision stays pending; a stale one is dismissed. In TypeScript `effect` is optional only so pre-C32 snapshots type-check, and absent is treated as `mayChange` | In-page consent must never authorise a change (D058, C32) |\n| P50 | A composer draft per conversation | One composer per tenant and workspace (by the stable workspace ID): its draft survives navigation, tenant round trips and the retirement of the conversation it was typed for. A message the core did not take is kept under \"Not sent\"; one whose delivery was not confirmed under \"Not confirmed\". Each send carries the presentation's own ID (`sendHarness.submission`, 32 hex): the core takes each ID at most once (a repeated delivery after a lost receipt sends nothing again) and reports the sends it took with the message each became and where each stands (`ConversationView.submissions`, newest 64): `pending` (taken, a sign-in renewal or the write confirmation under way: it may still be given back), `delivered` (its message entered the transcript, recorded by the engine session apart from the transcript's bounds) or `returned` (given back, or failed without an engine). Only `delivered` and `returned` settle anything; a tracked send whose conversation is no longer shown before its final word (retired, cleared, another tenant or workspace meanwhile) is kept as Not confirmed with its text. A \"Not confirmed\" record is settled only by its own conversation reporting its own ID: delivered, it leaves the list (and the box, if it holds exactly that text); given back, it leaves the list and its exact text stays in the box or is kept under Not sent, never cleared; an admitted send is likewise tracked until its conversation reports it, and its receipt never clears text the core already gave back, whichever arrives first; never by matching text, another send's entry, another conversation, or the transcript (which drops its oldest entries past 200 entries or 1 MiB). The core numbers a conversation's messages and never reuses an ID, also when restored. Nothing is sent by itself | A conversation's draft must stay recoverable after the core retires the conversation (C32 reviews) |\n| P51 | A new investigation prefilled with the ended conversation's opening question | The rail titles the current conversation from its kept opening too, so its title never changes as the transcript drops its oldest entries. A contextual arrival stays pending, bound to its tenant and workspace, until the conversation or the history resolves it (\"Finding that investigation…\" while the saved conversations are listed); listed without it, or not listable, the page says so; navigation, a new arrival, another workspace or a restart cancels it. The opening question is the conversation's opening message, whole, kept by the core apart from its title (a one-line, shortened label for lists): `ConversationView.opening` and `OpenedConversationView.opening`, captured when the first message (`u-1`) enters the transcript, saved with the record, restored with it and kept after the transcript drops its oldest entries. An archived conversation's record is read for it when the restart is asked for (nothing is filled in meanwhile); choosing another investigation, sending, opening a transcript, a new arrival or another workspace retires that read (a late result changes nothing); otherwise it settles by the core's receipt for the record (`HarnessHistoryView.lastOpen`: id, admission order, failure), newer than any open settled before it was asked for, never by a busy edge or a changed error. When it is not known (a record saved before C32 whose transcript no longer holds its first message) or the record cannot be read, the page says so and never fills in the title instead; a draft in the box is kept under Not sent and nothing is sent | The title is shortened (72 or 80 characters, one line) and lost the rest of a long or multi-line question; the attempt-12 review found the restart prefilling it |\n| P52 | The API Client re-skinned to the prototype's layout (request tree, tabs, method/URL line, 40% response pane) | The v2 API Client presents the C14/C15 workbench in the v2 layout through a layout context its wrapper provides (`shell/presentation.ts` `V2Layout`): the page heading becomes the v2 title row (no kicker), and the request editor puts the method, URL, Send and Save on one compact line with the request details beside a response pane (stacked when narrow). Collections, tabs, history, reviews and execution are the existing components and authority, unchanged; the legacy shell keeps its layout. Send's completion is announced in the editor's status region as \"<request> · <code> · <duration>\", the body outside it, in the response pane (known gap 5, V2-T14). The collection tree and tab strip keep the workbench's own arrangement | The workbench view itself is held by uncommitted owner work in this tree and is not edited; its layout is changed only through the components it renders |\n| P53 | \"Generic requests … refused by the core as today\" (screens/api-client-test-cases.md) | Unchanged C15 authority: a request to a known tenant operation that would change configuration needs its exact, natively confirmed review (`ReviewRequired`) before it is sent; an unknown operation at a registered origin is refused (`UnknownEndpoint`) | The screen text summarised both as refused; the core's existing rule is the authority and C33 does not change it |\n| P54 | Show the calls reveals each action's request and response \"as the diagnostics module already redacts them\" | The diagnostics module records typed events only. The core now records, in memory for the app run, each call it makes on a tenant's behalf through its transport (scans, management reads and changes with their token requests, AI Gateway inference): purpose, the credential by kind (the core attaches it), method, origin and path without the query (a query is marked withheld), header names (never values), request size, status or failure, duration and response content type, newest 50 per tenant (`ShellSnapshot.calls`, only while the preference is on; forgotten with the tenant), bounded as the contract accepts them (header names of at most 64 characters, at most 128, path cut at 2048 characters, method of at most 16, status 100-999, HTTPS origins only; `CALL_*_LIMIT`). Bodies are never kept: a response can carry an issued key or a token and requests carry tenant content. The API Client's own requests keep their existing redacted history. The preference is the operator's once chosen (`show_calls_chosen`), otherwise on until onboarding completes | The prototype has no calls panel; DESIGN-V2 §3 asked for it; secrets must never reach a snapshot (I6) |\n| P55 | Test Cases with per-case results and pack names (prototype `results`, `packCases`) | A case carries its starter pack's name (set when the bundled catalog's cases are added, at onboarding completion or by Add starter packs) and its last result: the verdict of a Playground scan opened from the case, recorded by the core when the scan settles and only while the case still holds the prompt and response that were scanned (`TestCase.result`, `Intent::Scan.case`). Neither is ever exported or imported (v1's file format is unchanged); a copy to another tenant drops the result. A case can be named by a scan only if it is a Runtime Security case of the tenant's open library (otherwise the scan is refused before anything is sent); a result stored on any other case is dropped when the library is read. Absent result: Not run. The library's workflows are kept in the v2 screen: New case, Add starter packs, Add public examples, Import…, Export…, Remove and explicit Copy to another tenant, with the library's unsaved, failure, problem and notice states and their recovery (Save again, Open the library again). Search and the product filter run over name, pack and input; pages of 20 (at most 32 cases per tenant, v1's limit) | The library had no result or pack; the prototype's results were UI state |\n| P56 | \"Open in Playground\" sets the Playground's prompt, direction and profile | It hands the case's profile, prompt, response and application to Administration's Runtime Playground (the v2 home of the Runtime scan form) and opens that tab; the operator runs the scan there, and that scan names the case. The scan form has no separate direction: a case's prompt and response are its directions. Nothing is scanned by opening it, and Inspect dispatches nothing | The Runtime scan form (C03) is the admitted execution surface; direction is expressed by which fields hold text |\n\nP10–P14 are the five round-16 Codex items carried as known gaps (see\n[DESIGN-V2.md](DESIGN-V2.md) \"Known gaps\"). P16–P21 were added after the C27\nattempt-01 review (R3–R6); P22–P23 after attempt-03 (F3a, F3b); P24–P25 were recorded in C28 and P26–P32 in C29 and P33–P38 in C30 and P39–P43 in C31, P44–P51 in C32 and P52–P56 in C33 when implementation met the host as it is (P26–P28 revised after the C29 attempt-01 review; P26–P27 again, and P32 added, after attempt-02; P33–P35 revised and P36–P38 added after the C30 attempt-01 review).\nOwner decision, 2026-10-25, recorded in C25 (\"drop it\" for each). The live\nrequirement of these checks is withdrawn from C25. Their source, fixture\nand native evidence stays required and is unchanged; each returns as a live\nrequirement in a later release once its cause is gone.\n\n| Row | Check | Why it could not pass |\n|---|---|---|\n| W49 | L11-account-switch | No second Academy test learner exists. Sign-in, protected admission and sign-out with revocation passed live |\n| W56 | L14-access-change-denied | Denial needs an entitled course whose access is withdrawn during the run; the course download under its lease and the local draft passed live |\n| W48 | L17-scope-change | Changing the shared `strata-cloud-manager` server's scope or grant is outside the `airs-v2-c25-*` change scope. Discovery, OAuth sign-in and revocation passed live; tool calls, approval, denial and refresh stay with the signed app (C26) |\n| W14 | L04-data-profile-replace | The DLP service answers HTTP 400 to every data profile and data pattern read or write by ID, predefined profiles included |\n| W15 | L04-filtering-profile-replace | The SDK request contract refuses the values the service stores (`direction` `c2s`/`s2c`, lower-case `log_severity`); an SDK follow-up. The app's own defect here (editing as read) was fixed and shown natively (C25-N01) |\n\n## D054 — scm-sdk 0.2.4\n\nOwner direction, 2026-10-25, recorded in C25 (\"my scm sdk is on 0.2.4 …\nupdate it\"). desktop-core pins `scm-sdk = \"=0.2.4\"` (crates.io), replacing\n0.1.0. The app built against it unchanged; the notice inventory was\nregenerated; every suite, the C25 gates and the operator-free live families\nran again on it. The upgrade did not change the two SDK contract\nmismatches C25 recorded (filtering profile `direction` and `log_severity`;\na cached Gateway stream chunk without `finish_reason`), which stay SDK\nfollow-ups.\n\n## D055 — C25 review attempt-01: deferrals and a waived cleanup\n\nOwner decisions, 2026-10-25, recorded after Codex C25 attempt-01.\n\n1. **L16 and L17's Harness-run checks are C26's.** L16 (the Harness over\n   Gateway inference, key and company sign-in, history and proposals; rows\n   W42–W45) and L17's MCP tool call, native approval, denial and refresh\n   (W48) run inside the Harness engine, which runs only in the signed app.\n   C25 does not claim them. They are required in C26's signed-app\n   acceptance and must pass there before a stable release; this is a\n   deferral, not a reduction.\n2. **The inactive `airs-v2-c25-profile` cleanup is waived.** The service\n   omits inactive profiles from every list and no endpoint finds one by\n   name; the owner looked in the console too. It is an inactive, unused\n   test profile in Redtail. The reconciliation log records the waiver.\n3. **W56's live explicit lab submit is withdrawn from C25.** It would\n   leave a graded submission on the owner's Academy account. Its source,\n   fixture and native evidence stay required; the download under lease,\n   the local draft and sign-out with revocation passed live.\n\n## D056 — Academy live follow-ups move to 2.1.0; stable is 2.0.0\n\nOwner decisions, 2026-10-25 (\"i don't care about the academy flows for\nnow, mark it as a followup in version 2.1.0, let's instead focus on\nshipping 2.0.0\"; the release version chosen as 2.0.0).\n\n1. **The stable release is 2.0.0.** The earlier 0.2.0 build, signed and\n   notarized, is the previous version for C26's manual update and\n   rollback checks.\n2. **C25-R2-F1 and C25-R2-G1 are 2.1.0 follow-ups.** They are interactive\n   live Academy flows for W53 (an exam started) and W65 (the Daily Incident\n   started and answered), and live runs asserting each Academy call's own\n   result. That result is the inline check's grading verdict, progress\n   cleared by a course reset, and certificates unchanged across it. The\n   runner already supports these assertions (`call.*` entries take\n   `?? <pointer> …` expectations, tested); the 2.1.0 work designates and\n   runs them. C25 stays complete at its D034 chunk gate. The P08 phase\n   sign-off does not raise C25 on these items.\n\n## D057 — C26 attempt 01: installed-acceptance defects and the Git rule\n\nC26 installed acceptance, attempt 01 (airsTest, 2026-10-26, record in\n`evidence/C26/installed/attempt-01/record.json`) passed install, signature,\nnotarization, staple, engine pin, update (U1, U2) and F01–F03 on the\nreal 2.0.0 bytes, and failed F04, F05 and F06. The tester stopped there;\nF07–F09, tamper and rollback did not run. C26 is not complete on this\nattempt; the defects are fixed, a new signed build is produced, and the\nwhole kit is run again.\n\n1. **F04 (Gateway Playground).** Text fields written without classes had\n   no visible edge after Tailwind's reset, so the endpoint, key and prompt\n   fields could not be seen. Fixed app-wide by a zero-specificity base\n   style for text fields, selects and text areas; the Run button now says\n   what is missing, and the Inference prerequisites tab reflects the\n   stored key instead of stale copy.\n2. **F05 (Harness company sign-in).** Company sign-in was reachable only\n   after tenant, management client and Gateway workspace, and nothing\n   before that mentioned it. The Harness now shows a \"what you need first\"\n   checklist, an always-visible company sign-in section (disabled with its\n   reason until a workspace is chosen) and specific save refusals.\n3. **F06 (API Client).** Revises the D040 rule \"a new collection\n   is created only inside a Git repository (v1)\": Git is optional. A new\n   collection can be created in any folder the user chooses; opening an\n   empty folder initialises a collection there; a non-empty folder without\n   `collection.toml` is still refused, now visibly, with a next step. The\n   Git tab works unchanged when the collection is inside a repository.\n\n## D058 — UI v2 \"Refined Native\": frozen prototype, P09 extraction plan\n\nAccepted by the owner on 2026-10-26 (\"I accept it as written\"), with authority to implement P09 end to end autonomously and to proceed with C28 while C26 remains in progress; every chunk is reviewed until it scores 9 in every dimension.\n\nThe owner explored fifteen interactive shell concepts on a Claude Design canvas\n(https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL), selected \"Refined\nNative\", and iterated its prototype (R2App, R2Onboard, R2Harness ×3,\nR2Rationale) through sixteen independent Codex usability reviews (scores 6, 7,\n7, then 8 on rounds 4–16). The prototype is frozen at canvas version 134 and\ncopied verbatim into `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` with a SHA-256\nmanifest; the canvas page is not changed again. Prototype data is invented and\nis never shipped.\n\nDecisions (grilling session, 55 questions, all recommendations accepted):\n\n1. Extraction is spec, then TypeScript presentation, then Rust contract, as\n   phase P09 chunks C27–C34 (see the chunk index). The first implementation\n   slice is the shell plus the first-run wizard and add-tenant sheet.\n2. The prototype's state model is normative for twelve named invariants\n   (`docs/architecture/ui-v2/invariants.md`) and illustrative for code shape.\n   Every prototype state field is assigned Rust-owned/observed or UI-transient\n   (`state-ownership.md`); nothing goes to browser storage.\n3. D014 holds: secrets enter only through the host secret sheet and vault;\n   an issued API key is disclosed only through the existing `RevealSecret`\n   host path, held for the active context and never persisted; key rotation\n   is the admitted sequence additional key (`Version`) → local deployment\n   acknowledgement → old key `Delete` with readback (no invented grace\n   period or revocation); typed-name confirmation is validated in the core;\n   tenant switch is refused by the core while a Harness decision is pending;\n   write-capable tool calls use the native confirmation sheet.\n3a. Only products with an admitted service are verified, inventoried or\n   changed (Runtime Security, Gateway, Red Team and Model/skill reads).\n   Agentic Identity Security and Agentic Endpoint Security render as \"Not\n   available in this release\" with a dependency gate; no inventory,\n   verification result or mutation is invented for them.\n4. Tokens live in a local `--v2-*` file; Academy variables stay for the\n   embedded learner view. One Rust navigation inventory feeds the native menu,\n   sidebar and palette; ⌘1–⌘9 are menu-defined. Feature ids gain `home` and\n   `inbox`.\n5. The new shell ships behind a Rust-owned `ui_variant` flag and the legacy\n   shell, flag and legacy acceptance scripts are removed in C34. The Academy\n   learner view is embedded unchanged.\n6. The wizard realises D013 (scan key first and only required credential);\n   v1 import is offered on the tenant step.\n7. Verification: Vitest fixtures named after every prototype wrapper board,\n   Rust tests per invariant, native acceptance extended with focus ring, live\n   regions, topmost modal and Reduce Motion persistence, screenshot evidence\n   at 100%/200% in both themes per chunk. Live calls only in C29 (verify,\n   Vulture, reads) and C31 (review apply, Redtail, prefixed) inside the scope\n   designated 2026-10-25. C27 is reviewed at 9/10 in every dimension and the\n   controller enforces it: a `docs_only` chunk has no native preparation or\n   acceptance and refuses owner acceptance at completion and at recognition;\n   C28–C34 follow D034.\n8. Minimum window 1180×720 with the rail collapsing below 1280 wide; toasts\n   persist under Reduce Motion; \"Show the calls\" panel shows diagnostics-\n   redacted wire data; English only in P09; chunks commit on main except C34.\n9. C27 proceeds in parallel with C26 (docs and controller registration only);\n   C28 waits for C26.\n\n\n## D059 — P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen at 8\n\nAccepted by the owner on 2026-10-29 after a grilling session (23 questions, all\naccepted; `docs/planning/p09-recut.md`). C32 had taken twenty review rounds\nwhile the built v2 screens never matched the design: chunks had wrapped the\nlegacy feature components in v2 frames instead of porting the prototype.\n\n- The frozen prototype (D058, version 134) is the specification per screen; the\n  canvas is not consulted (it is unreachable from the implementing account).\n- A screen is done when it matches its board side by side at 1440×900 in both\n# P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen\n\nAgreed with the owner on 2026-10-29 after C32 took twenty review rounds while\nthe built v2 screens never matched the design. This supersedes the chunking\nof C33–C34 in `IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md`; the specification\n(`docs/architecture/ui-v2/`) and the core work of C27–C33 stand.\n\n## The bar\n\n- **The prototype is the spec.** `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` (Refined\n  Native v2, version 134, checksums in `MANIFEST.sha256`) is the reference for\n  every screen; the canvas is not consulted.\n- **A screen is done when it matches its board.** Side by side at 1440 wide in\n  light and dark (the board is drawn at 1440×900; where the display cannot\n  give a 900px web area both sides are rendered at the window's measured web\n  area, named in the composite's caption, 1440×800 on the current Mac), with\n  real core data wired, and the fast checks (typecheck,\n  Vitest, the affected Rust suites) passing. Parity is structural: regions,\n  widths, spacing, type scale, tokens and component shapes. Content differs;\n  accessibility corrections (focus rings, contrast, status regions) may deviate\n  and are noted. The owner judges each composite.\n- **Composites** live at `docs/planning/evidence/<chunk>/parity/<board>-<theme>.png`\n  (prototype left, app right) with `notes.md` listing deviations, produced by\n  `tools/parity/compose.py` (headless render of the prototype board beside the\n  fixture app's own capture).\n- **One heavy gate per chunk**, after the owner's parity approval: rebuild,\n  native acceptance, the full Rust fixture suite and the Codex review, accepted\n  at **8**. The phase review (after C44) is **9**; the C29 live Vulture row runs\n  before it once the owner designates the L19 family.\n- **No legacy views inside v2.** Each screen is new components over the\n  existing core intents and `ShellSnapshot`; the legacy feature components stay\n  untouched for the legacy shell until C44 removes it.\n- **Cadence.** The shell is approved before any screen inside it. After that\n  a chunk's composite is posted and work moves to the next chunk; the owner's\n  notes are applied as they arrive.\n\n## Chunks\n\n| Chunk | Screen | Board(s) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C33 | core: case results and packs, call log, submissions | — | committed without a receipt; its two screens are superseded by C40/C41 |\n| C35 | Shell | R2App any start | 56px header (title, 260px search, Explain switch = `showCalls`, Harness button); nav with product children and badges; status block; tenant menu 360px; palette 640px; tokens: text 13px, gaps, scrims, header/section/overlay sizes; Classic switch moves to Settings |\n| C36 | Home | start=home | 3-column product cards with sparklines; Needs attention beside Quick actions |\n| C37 | Inbox | start=inbox | fix-up: 430px list column, 3-column facts grid |\n| C38 | Harness | start=harness (+ convo boards) | title band; 244px rail; transcript with 46px composer; 350px Decisions aside; own setup panels |\n| C39a | Administration: Runtime Security | start=admin | section + tab strip; overview stat grid with sparkline; Playground form; generic tables over the management reads |\n| C39b | Administration: other products | start=admin, start=review | the remaining products on the same pattern; the review sheet (620px) |\n| C40 | Test Cases | start=cases | explain banner; one table card; packs and new-case dialogs (560px); detail sheet (520px) |\n| C41 | API Client | start=api | full port: title row with chips; 286px request tree; open-request tabs; request sub-tabs; response pane |\n| C42 | Academy | start=academy | prototype chrome around the hosted learner embed; composite judged on the chrome |\n| C43 | Wizard | R2Onboard (all steps, mode=add) | re-port of the presentation over the existing state machine: pinned footer, 320/244px rail, section cards, segmented control, switch; model-driven extras housed in the prototype's cards |\n| C44 | Legacy shell removal + phase review | — | on a branch; then the P09 review at 9 |\n\nEach chunk brief under `docs/planning/chunks/` names the board, the regions\nto port, the core intents and snapshot fields it reads, the native checks and\nthe test ids. The controller registers C35–C44 with a bar of 8.\n\n## Fast loop\n\nWhile building: `pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec tsc --noEmit`,\n`vitest run` for the desktop app, and `cargo test -p desktop-core --features\ntest-fixtures --test <suite>` for suites the change touches. The rebuild and\nnative run happen only at the gate, or when the composite needs a fresh capture.\nReviews run from a separate worktree so work continues meanwhile.\n\n## First moves\n\n1. Commit C33 as it stands (core, tests, the superseded screens).\n2. Commit this re-cut: chunk briefs, controller registration, checks.\n3. Build `tools/parity/compose.py` and run it on the current shell: the first\n   composite is the baseline gap.\n4. C35.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '830,975p' crates/desktop-core/src/authority/harness_domain.rs\n sed -n '250,405p' crates/desktop-core/src/harness/tools.rs\n rg -n 'ApprovalEffect|needs_decision|DesktopRead|desktopRead|MayChange|ReadOnly' crates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs crates/desktop-core/src/authority/harness_tools.rs\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '830,975p' crates/desktop-core/src/authority/harness_domain.rs\n sed -n '250,405p' crates/desktop-core/src/harness/tools.rs\n rg -n 'ApprovalEffect|needs_decision|DesktopRead|desktopRead|MayChange|ReadOnly' crates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs crates/desktop-core/src/authority/harness_tools.rs\"","aggregated_output":"            .as_ref()\n            .filter(|c| c.ended.is_none() && c.stop.is_none() && self.harness_bound(c))\n            .map(|c| c.progress_approvals())\n            .unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    /// Ends the conversation and drops it: nothing of it is shown or kept\n    /// (C30 \"discard and switch\"). Its protected history record is removed\n    /// and no later save of it (the turn the stop cuts short) is admitted.\n    pub(super) fn discard_harness(&mut self, id: &OperationId) -> Result<(), Refusal> {\n        let root = self.history_root();\n        let conversation = self.live_harness(id)?;\n        if let Some(job) = conversation.write_confirm.take() {\n            conversation.keep_unsent(job);\n        }\n        conversation.stop = Some(HarnessStop::Ended);\n        conversation.retired = true;\n        conversation.cancel.cancel();\n        let history_id = conversation.history_id.clone();\n        let scope = root.map(|root| {\n            Scope::new(\n                &root,\n                conversation.tenant.as_str(),\n                &conversation.workspace.id,\n            )\n        });\n        if let Some(scope) = scope {\n            self.discard_history(scope, history_id);\n        }\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    /// Ends the conversation: the engine is stopped and reaped.\n    pub(super) fn end_harness(&mut self, id: &OperationId) -> Result<(), Refusal> {\n        let conversation = self.live_harness(id)?;\n        if let Some(job) = conversation.write_confirm.take() {\n            conversation.keep_unsent(job);\n        }\n        conversation.stop = Some(HarnessStop::Ended);\n        conversation.cancel.cancel();\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    /// Stops whatever conversation runs (sleep, quit), keeping it shown.\n    pub(super) fn stop_harness(&mut self, why: HarnessStop) -> bool {\n        match self.harness.as_mut().filter(|c| c.ended.is_none()) {\n            Some(c) => {\n                // A renewal waiting for changes to be allowed again is not\n                // sent; its message goes back to the box (P06 review R3-F1).\n                if let Some(job) = c.write_confirm.take() {\n                    c.keep_unsent(job);\n                }\n                c.stop.get_or_insert(why);\n                c.cancel.cancel();\n                true\n            }\n            None => false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Clears an ended conversation (nothing of it is kept).\n    pub(super) fn clear_harness(&mut self, id: &OperationId) -> Result<(), Refusal> {\n        if !self\n            .harness\n            .as_ref()\n            .is_some_and(|c| &c.id == id && c.ended.is_some())\n        {\n            return Err(Refusal::InvalidInput);\n        }\n        self.harness = None;\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    /// The conversation's task returned.\n    pub(super) fn harness_settled(&mut self, id: &OperationId, outcome: Outcome) {\n        if let Some(c) = self.harness.as_mut().filter(|c| &c.id == id) {\n            c.ended = Some(outcome);\n            // Ended without being asked to and without failing: its\n            // eight-hour bound passed.\n            let failed = c\n                .progress\n                .lock()\n                .map(|p| p.failure.is_some())\n                .unwrap_or(true);\n            if c.stop.is_none() && !c.retired && !failed {\n                c.stop = Some(HarnessStop::Deadline);\n            }\n            if c.retired {\n                self.harness = None;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Whether the conversation still belongs to the active context, the\n    /// chosen workspace and the key it was started with.\n    fn harness_bound(&self, c: &Conversation) -> bool {\n        let Some(tenant) = self.tenant(&c.tenant) else {\n            return false;\n        };\n        // The credential chosen for its workspace now, and its identity.\n        let sign_in = tenant\n            .harness_sign_in\n            .as_ref()\n            .filter(|s| s.selected && s.target.workspace.id == c.workspace.id);\n        let credential_ok = match (&c.grant, sign_in) {\n            (Some(grant), Some(s)) => s\n                .grant\n                .as_ref()\n                .is_some_and(|g| g.id == grant.id && s.target.endpoint == c.endpoint),\n            (None, None) => matches!(\n                &tenant.inference,\n                ScanCapability::Ready { record, .. } if Some(record) == c.record.as_ref()\n            ),\n            _ => false,\n        };\n        // The project and its access as selected now (C21).\n        let project = tenant.harness_project.as_ref();\n        let project_ok = project.map(|p| &p.path) == c.project.as_ref()\n            && project.is_some_and(|p| p.write) == c.write;\n        // Its MCP servers: still on, with the same sign-ins and revisions\n        // (C22). An expired sign-in alone keeps it, and so does a catalog\n        // that is loading or could not be read (nothing remote runs then).\n        let mcp_ok = self.mcp_scope_check(&c.tenant, &c.workspace.id, &c.mcp)\n            != super::harness_mcp::ScopeCheck::Changed;\n        !c.retired\n            && credential_ok\n            && project_ok\n            && mcp_ok\n            && Some(&c.context) == self.active_ref.as_ref()\n            && self.workspace_current(Some(&c.reference))\n    }\n\n    /// Retires a conversation that is no longer bound (run on every change).\n    pub(super) fn sync_harness(&mut self) {\n        // A confirmation whose decision is gone is ended (C32).\n        self.retire_harness_confirmation();\n        let bound = self.harness.as_ref().is_some_and(|c| self.harness_bound(c));\n        let Some(c) = self.harness.as_mut() else {\n            return;\n        };\n        if !bound {\n            c.retired = true;\n            c.cancel.cancel();\n            if c.ended.is_some() {\n                self.harness = None;\n            }\n            \"inventory\" => {\n                let a: InventoryArgs = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                let limit = a.limit.unwrap_or(25);\n                if !offered(a.resource)\n                    || !(1..=MAX_TOOL_PAGE).contains(&limit)\n                    || a.offset > 1_000_000\n                {\n                    return None;\n                }\n                Self::Inventory {\n                    resource: a.resource,\n                    offset: a.offset,\n                    limit,\n                }\n            }\n            \"detail\" => {\n                let a: DetailArgs = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                (offered(a.resource) && plain(&a.id)).then_some(Self::Detail {\n                    resource: a.resource,\n                    id: a.id,\n                })?\n            }\n            \"session\" => {\n                let a: SessionArgs = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                (plain(&a.session_id) && plain(&a.app_id) && plain(&a.app_name)).then_some(\n                    Self::Session {\n                        session_id: a.session_id,\n                        app_id: a.app_id,\n                        app_name: a.app_name,\n                    },\n                )?\n            }\n            \"search\" => {\n                let a: SearchArgs = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                plain(&a.query).then_some(Self::Search { query: a.query })?\n            }\n            \"redTeam\" => {\n                let a: RedTeamArgs = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                matches!(a.window, 7 | 15 | 30).then_some(Self::RedTeam { window: a.window })?\n            }\n            \"supplyChain\" => {\n                let _: Empty = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                Self::SupplyChain\n            }\n            \"scan\" => {\n                let a: ScanArgs = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                let ok = |t: &Option<String>| t.as_ref().is_none_or(|t| t.len() <= MAX_SCAN_TEXT);\n                let any = a.prompt.as_ref().is_some_and(|t| !t.trim().is_empty())\n                    || a.response.as_ref().is_some_and(|t| !t.trim().is_empty());\n                (any && plain(&a.profile) && ok(&a.prompt) && ok(&a.response)).then_some(\n                    Self::Scan {\n                        profile: a.profile,\n                        prompt: a.prompt,\n                        response: a.response,\n                    },\n                )?\n            }\n            \"cases\" => {\n                let _: Empty = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                Self::Cases\n            }\n            \"mcpServers\" => {\n                let _: Empty = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                Self::McpServers\n            }\n            \"proposeChange\" => {\n                let a: ProposeArgs = serde_json::from_value(args).ok()?;\n                // The Runtime and DLP kinds (create, update, delete); an API\n                // key's version, the start of a rotation the operator\n                // finishes in Administration (C32); and a guardrail of the\n                // conversation's own Gateway workspace, created or updated,\n                // reviewed under that workspace (C32). Nothing else.\n                let runtime = matches!(\n                    a.kind,\n                    ChangeKind::Profiles\n                        | ChangeKind::Topics\n                        | ChangeKind::Applications\n                        | ChangeKind::ApiKeys\n                        | ChangeKind::DataPatterns\n                        | ChangeKind::DataProfiles\n                        | ChangeKind::FilteringProfiles\n                        | ChangeKind::Dictionaries\n                ) && matches!(\n                    a.action,\n                    ChangeAction::Create | ChangeAction::Update | ChangeAction::Delete\n                );\n                let rotation = a.kind == ChangeKind::ApiKeys && a.action == ChangeAction::Version;\n                let guardrail = a.kind == ChangeKind::GatewayGuardrails\n                    && matches!(a.action, ChangeAction::Create | ChangeAction::Update);\n                if !(runtime || rotation || guardrail)\n                    || a.summary.trim().is_empty()\n                    || a.summary.chars().count() > 512\n                    || a.target.len() > 256\n                    || a.target.chars().any(char::is_control)\n                {\n                    return None;\n                }\n                let body = Value::Object(a.body.unwrap_or_default()).to_string();\n                Self::ProposeChange {\n                    kind: a.kind,\n                    action: a.action,\n                    target: a.target,\n                    body,\n                    summary: a.summary.chars().filter(|c| !c.is_control()).collect(),\n                }\n            }\n            _ => return None,\n        };\n        Some(call)\n    }\n\n    /// Whether the call waits for the operator's decision in the page\n    /// (C32): every read of the tenant does; staging a proposal reads\n    /// nothing and only ever opens the operator's own review.\n    pub fn needs_decision(&self) -> bool {\n        !matches!(self, Self::ProposeChange { .. })\n    }\n\n    /// The tool's name, as the engine called it.\n    pub fn name(&self) -> &'static str {\n        match self {\n            Self::Inventory { .. } => \"inventory\",\n            Self::Detail { .. } => \"detail\",\n            Self::Session { .. } => \"session\",\n            Self::Search { .. } => \"search\",\n            Self::RedTeam { .. } => \"redTeam\",\n            Self::SupplyChain => \"supplyChain\",\n            Self::Scan { .. } => \"scan\",\n            Self::Cases => \"cases\",\n            Self::McpServers => \"mcpServers\",\n            Self::ProposeChange { .. } => \"proposeChange\",\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// A short, model-free description for the transcript.\n    pub fn title(&self) -> String {\n        match self {\n            Self::Inventory { resource, .. } => {\n                format!(\"Read the {} inventory\", resource_name(*resource))\n            }\n            Self::Detail { resource, id } => format!(\"Read {} {id}\", resource_name(*resource)),\n            Self::Session { session_id, .. } => format!(\"Read session {session_id}\"),\n            Self::Search { query } => format!(\"Searched sessions for {query}\"),\n            Self::RedTeam { window } => format!(\"Read AI Red Teaming ({window} days)\"),\n            Self::SupplyChain => \"Read Model Security and AgentGuard\".to_owned(),\n            Self::Scan { .. } => \"Scanned text with Runtime Security\".to_owned(),\n            Self::Cases => \"Read the test case library\".to_owned(),\n            Self::McpServers => \"Read the workspace's MCP servers\".to_owned(),\n            Self::ProposeChange { summary, .. } => format!(\"Proposed a change: {summary}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn resource_name(resource: ManagementResource) -> &'static str {\n    match resource {\n        ManagementResource::Profiles => \"profiles\",\ncrates/desktop-core/src/authority/harness_tools.rs:238:            && pending.effect == crate::harness::ApprovalEffect::MayChange\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:30:    ApprovalEffect, ApprovalKind, Cleanup, EntryState, HarnessApproval, HarnessEntry,\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1321:                    if kind == ApprovalKind::DesktopRead {\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1565:        if call.needs_decision() {\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1574:                kind: ApprovalKind::DesktopRead,\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1583:                effect: ApprovalEffect::ReadOnly,\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1598:                .push((token.clone(), id.clone(), ApprovalKind::DesktopRead));\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1668:            ApprovalKind::McpTool | ApprovalKind::DesktopRead => false,\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1711:                ApprovalEffect::ReadOnly\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1713:                ApprovalEffect::MayChange\ncrates/desktop-core/src/harness/session.rs:1780:            effect: ApprovalEffect::MayChange,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
