{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'P4[4-9]|P50|P51' docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md\n cat docs/planning/evidence/C31/remediation-attempt-05/reload-lifecycle/old-red-build-01.log docs/planning/evidence/C31/remediation-attempt-05/reload-lifecycle/new-green-02.log\n rg -n 'ui_v2_harness|test result:|renderer_review_lifecycle' docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs/attempt-09/core-fixtures.log docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs/attempt-09/host.log | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"57:| P44 | Lifecycle conversations are seeded data in the prototype (`CONVOS`) | The three lifecycles are fixtures in the contracts package (`fixtures/wire/harness-lifecycle-{blocks,mcp,keys}.json`), one `ConversationView` per step (asking, approved, declined, ended) plus the saved transcript. Each read is one of the desktop's own read tools (`desktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`): blocks `search`, mcp `mcpServers`, keys `inventory` of API keys. They are regression inputs and boards only: a real tenant never shows them (I12) | The core decides approvals; fixtures must be shapes the core emits, validated by `parseShellSnapshot` |\n58:| P45 | The mcp and keys follow-ups open a guardrail review and the key rotation flow | `proposeChange` admits, besides the Runtime and DLP kinds, an API key `version` (the hand-off of a rotation: it opens the C31 review sheet and its staged sequence) and a guardrail of the conversation's own Gateway workspace (create or update only), staged with that workspace's reference so its review is bound to it; Gateway deletes and every other Gateway change, and a key rotation in place, stay refused. The follow-up of a key hand-off shows the rotation stage from `ShellSnapshot.rotations`. The mcp lifecycle's guardrail is described as what it is (a pii-check guardrail on all messages while the unlisted server is reviewed); no guardrail expresses an MCP allow list | A proposal only ever opens the operator's own review (D014); the follow-ups must be core-owned, not page-invented |\n59:| P46 | `endConversation` returns an archive id | `endHarness` keeps its receipt; the ended conversation names its saved transcript (`ConversationView.historyId`, the protected history record, shown only with save evidence: `savedAt` set and no `saveFailure`) and the rail marks it ended; \"Open the saved transcript\" opens that record | The archive already exists as the C20 protected history; a second id would duplicate it |\n60:| P47 | The mcp lifecycle reads `GatewayRead::McpServers` (P16) | A new desktop read tool `mcpServers` reads the conversation workspace's MCP catalog (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, the read the MCP panel uses) for the engine, decided in the page like every desktop read | The prototype's AES inventory has no admitted service; the Gateway catalog is the admitted read P16 named |\n61:| P48 | Read-only tool calls are approved in the page (C21 assumed) | Every desktop read tool (inventory, detail, session, search, Red Teaming, model observation, scan, test cases, MCP catalog) now waits for the operator's decision in the page (`ApprovalKind::DesktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`); declined, nothing is read and the engine is told so. Staging a proposal reads nothing and still runs at once. Before C32 these reads ran without asking | The lifecycles' approve and decline paths, and the operator's control over what tenant data reaches the model |\n62:| P49 | The core decides from the tool's write flag whether the native confirmation is required (C21 assumed) | No such branch existed: every decision was in-page. The core now classifies each decision (`HarnessApproval.effect`): a desktop read, or a command while the project is read-only, is `readOnly`; a file change, a command with changes allowed and any workspace MCP tool (whose effect the desktop cannot know; tools carry no trusted annotations) is `mayChange`, and allowing it opens the native confirmation (`Effect::ConfirmHarness`), who