 claim notes that quote 'ignore previous instructions'.\", 'state': 'complete'}, {'id': 'u-1/a-2', 'role': 'assistant', 'text': \"Recommendation: narrow the injection-override topic's examples. I staged that change for your review; it is not applied.\", 'state': 'complete'}]\nended {'phase': 'ended', 'historyId': '5a0e2c7b-1d3f-4a6b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d', 'savedAt': 1793230000, 'saveFailure': None}\npackages/desktop-contracts/fixtures/wire/harness-lifecycle-mcp.json\nsource None None\nquestion Which laptops run an MCP server that is not on the allow list?\nasking [{'id': '1b2c3d4e5f60718293a4b5c6', 'kind': 'desktopRead', 'command': None, 'cwd': None, 'reason': None, 'files': [], 'server': 'airs_desktop', 'tool': 'mcpServers', 'arguments': '{}', 'effect': 'readOnly'}]\napproved followups [{'id': 'P-1', 'summary': 'Deny requests carrying personal data in this workspace (pii-check, all messages)', 'draft': {'kind': 'gatewayGuardrails', 'action': 'create', 'target': '', 'workspace': 'gw-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa', 'body': '{\"name\":\"pii-deny-all-messages\",\"checks\":[{\"id\":\"pii-check\",\"parameters\":{\"scan_scope\":\"all_messages\"}}],\"actions\":{\"deny\":true}}'}, 'state': 'staged', 'review': None}]\napproved assistant [{'id': 'u-1/a-1', 'role': 'assistant', 'text': \"The workspace's MCP catalog lists three servers. 'scratch-tools' has no sign-in configured and is not one your team listed as approved. The desktop reads this workspace's catalog, not laptops: per-endpoint data needs an admitted Agentic Endpoint Security service, which this release does not have.\", 'state': 'complete'}, {'id': 'u-1/a-2', 'role': 'assistant', 'text': \"Recommendation: until 'scratch-tools' is reviewed, add a workspace guardrail that denies requests carrying personal data (the pii-check guardrail on all messages). I staged it for your review in this workspace; it is not applied. It does not block an MCP server by itself: removing 'scratch-tools' from the workspace is done in AI Gateway.\", 'state': 'complete'}]\nended {'phase': 'ended', 'historyId': '6b1f3d8c-2e4a-4b7c-9d0e-1f2a3b4c5d6e', 'savedAt': 1793230000, 'saveFailure': None}\npackages/desktop-contracts/fixtures/wire/harness-lifecycle-keys.json\nsource None None\nquestion Which API keys expire before the audit, and can you rotate them?\nasking [{'id': '2c3d4e5f60718293a4b5c6d7', 'kind': 'desktopRead', 'command': None, 'cwd': None, 'reason': None, 'files': [], 'server': 'airs_desktop', 'tool': 'inventory', 'arguments': '{\"resource\":\"apiKeys\",\"limit\":25}', 'effect': 'readOnly'}]\napproved followups [{'id': 'P-1', 'summary': \"Start the rotation of support-copilot's key in Administration\", 'draft': {'kind': 'apiKeys', 'action': 'version', 'target': 'key-1', 'body': '{\"cust_app\":\"support-copilot\",\"cust_env\":\"production\",\"cust_cloud_provider\":\"aws\",\"api_key_name\":\"Support key 2\",\"dp_name\":\"dp-1\",\"revoked\":false,\"rotation_time_interval\":30,\"rotation_time_unit\":\"days\"}'}, 'state': 'staged', 'review': None}]\napproved assistant [{'id': 'u-1/a-1', 'role': 'assistant', 'text': \"One key expires before the audit: 'Support key' of support-copilot, on 2026-11-20. I will not rotate it: rotation is a write; it goes through Administration and a review.\", 'state': 'complete'}, {'id': 'u-1/a-2', 'role': 'assistant', 'text': 'I staged the start of its rotation (an additional key for support-copilot) for your review in Administration. Nothing is issued until you confirm it there.', 'state': 'complete'}]\nended {'phase': 'ended', 'historyId': '7c2a4e9d-3f5b-4c8d-8e1f-2a3b4c5d6e7f', 'savedAt': 1793230000, 'saveFailure': None}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; s=Path(\\\"docs/planning/decisions.md\\\").read_text(); sections=s.split(\\\"\\\\n## \\\"); [print(\\\"## \\\"+x) for x in sections if x.startswith((\\\"D010 \\\",\\\"D014 \\\",\\\"D015 \\\",\\\"D016 \\\",\\\"D046 \\\",\\\"D047 \\\",\\\"D048 \\\",\\\"D049 \\\"))]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; s=Path(\\\"docs/planning/decisions.md\\\").read_text(); sections=s.split(\\\"\\\\n## \\\"); [print(\\\"## \\\"+x) for x in sections if x.startswith((\\\"D010 \\\",\\\"D014 \\\",\\\"D015 \\\",\\\"D016 \\\",\\\"D046 \\\",\\\"D047 \\\",\\\"D048 \\\",\\\"D049 \\\"))]'\"","aggregated_output":"## D010 — No bundled Node/Bun backend runtime\n\nAccepted in interview round 3, Q10.\n\nThe shipped application contains no Node/Bun runtime or TypeScript backend\nworkers, including Academy, Harness, and MCP authentication workers. React's\nJavaScript runs in the WebView. Build-time JavaScript tooling is distinct from a\nshipped backend runtime.\n\nPreserve the existing native Rust Harness engine and evaluate whether the managed\nCLI can be omitted after replacing host workers and resealing the package. Do not\ninfer that a full CLI or agent-engine rewrite is necessary. User-approved external\ndevelopment tools are a separate concern, not permission to smuggle a required\nbackend runtime into the distribution.\n\n## D014 — Rust application authority\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q14.\n\nRust owns tenant context, credential access, request lifecycles, and permission\nto apply configuration changes. React presents drafts, reviews, and results.\nConfiguration review binds the tenant, credentials, resource, and draft; changed\ncontext invalidates it. Harness proposals enter the same operator review flow\nand cannot authorize Apply. Initially preserve the existing Harness project/tool\nrestrictions; expansion would require a separate explicit design decision.\n\n## D015 — Import work, reestablish authentication\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q15.\n\nImport selected user-authored work and settings, including saved cases, reports,\nAcademy drafts, API Client projects, and historical conversations. Require fresh\ncredential setup and sign-in. Regenerate disposable caches/downloads. Imported\nconversations are inspectable history until resumption compatibility is validated;\nold pending approvals and authentication sessions never transfer.\n\nReading protected source work for an explicitly selected import is distinct from\ntransferring its old authentication credentials. Define restartable import\nreceipts, version/account checks, rollback, and source-preservation tests.\n\n## D016 — Explicit offline behavior\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q16.\n\nOffline mode supports local drafts, saved work, history, and valid downloaded\nAcademy content. Online operations require explicit retry after reconnection.\nDo not silently replay configuration changes, scans, grading submissions, or\nother remote actions. Preserve unknown/uncertain outcomes rather than asserting\nthat cancellation or disconnection undid remote work.\n\n## D046 — The native Harness engine: sealed resource, lifecycle and the C19 conversation\n\nDecided in C19 (implementer decision for the C19 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**The engine and its seal.** The engine is the pinned build of\n`prisma-airs-harness` 9331a58 (protocol `airs-desktop/1`, runtime\n0.1.7-desktop.2). It is not rebuilt here. It is the binary v1 sealed at v1\ncommit 74754c02: `packaging/harness/darwin-arm64.tar.gz`, a Git LFS object\nwith sha256 e79f564a…. That binary's SHA-256 (f8e49cee…) matches its\n`BUILD-INFO.json`, which names the source commit 9331a58 and the build\ncommand, and v1's runtime pin.\n\n`tools/harness/seal.py` reseals it for v2 and takes only the engine and\nits notices:\n- `airs-harness`;\n- `BUILD-INFO.json`, `DEPENDENCIES.json`, `LICENSE` and `NOTICE`;\n- `licenses/engine/`, the engine's dependencies' licenses.\n\nNode, the launcher scripts, the managed CLI and its `node_modules` are\nleft out. The output is deterministic. It consists of:\n- `resources/harness/darwin-arm64.tar.gz` (Git LFS, 113 MB): the files\n  above and a `MANIFEST.json` inventory;\n- `resources/harness/pin.json`: the pin record, compiled into\n  desktop-core.\n\n`xtask native-prepare` extracts the archive and checks every file against\nthe inventory and the pin. A Tauri overlay of its own,\n`tauri.harness.conf.json`, bundles it as `Contents/Resources/harness/`; the\nfixture overlay still only renames the identity. Signed release packaging\nis C26.\n\n**Verification on every start.** Before a conversation reads its key or\nlaunches anything, the bundled executable must be:\n- an absolute path named `airs-harness`;\n- a regular file, not a link;\n- unchanged while it is read (same device, inode, size and time);\n- the pinned SHA-256.\n\nA changed or missing engine fails as `engineUnavailable`. Nothing is\nstarted and no key is read. The check is part of the measured start\n(`harness-start-ready`, C19 workload).\n\n**Lifecycle (DESIGN §3).** desktop-core owns the conversation policy.\ndesktop-host owns the OS adapter: `sidecar::OsLauncher` spawns, owns the\nprocess group and reaps. The engine starts with:\n- no inherited environment;\n- the argument `desktop-server` only;\n- stdin and stdout piped, and stderr discarded, so raw engine diagnostics\n  never reach a view or a log;\n- its own process group;\n- a fresh `0700` runtime home, `<app data>/harness-runtime/run-<hex>`, as\n  its `AIRS_HARNESS_HOME`.\n\nThe environment it gets is exactly:\n- `PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin` and `LANG`;\n- `TMPDIR`, inside the home;\n- `AIRS_HARNESS_HOME`;\n- `CODEX_CA_CERTIFICATE`: a file in the home holding the app transport\n  policy's additional roots, only when there are any;\n- the transport policy's proxy mode: `NO_PROXY=*` for Direct, the\n  explicit proxy URL (never one with credentials), or nothing for\n  System.\n\nThe key reaches the engine only in the bootstrap frame on stdin, encoded\ninto one zeroizing buffer within the 64 KiB frame bound.\n\nStopping closes stdin, waits 2 s, kills the process group, waits 2 s, and\nreaps. Exit is observed without reaping (`waitid` with `WNOWAIT`), so the\ngroup is killed while it is still the engine's, and nothing is signalled\nafter reaping. The engine runs a supervisor process of its own, which\ngoes with the group. A process that cannot be reaped is\n`cleanupFailed`, never success. The home is then removed; at a start,\nhomes left by an earlier run are removed too.\n\n**Binding and retirement.** A conversation is admitted exactly as a\nPlayground run is (D044):\n- the current context;\n- the chosen Gateway workspace's reference;\n- the inference key stored for that workspace, sent only to the runtime\n  endpoint stored with it.\n\nThe engine's identifiers are UUIDs. The workspace's Gateway ID is one.\nThe tenant and credential IDs are derived UUIDs (SHA-256 of a purpose and\nthe native ID, shaped as version 8). The credential epoch is 1 until C20\nbrings refresh epochs. The receipt must echo the binding exactly, with no\nMCP server.\n\nA conversation is its own task, not one of the four operation slots,\nbecause it may last hours (at most eight). It is retired the moment it is\nno longer bound, which every published change checks: another context,\nanother workspace, or the key replaced or removed. Its view is withdrawn\nat once, and its engine is stopped and reaped. Ending it, sleep\n(NSWorkspace will-sleep) and quitting also stop it; quitting waits up to\n5 s for the reap.\n\n**The C19 conversation.** C19 runs a read-only conversation:\n- no project and no local environment;\n- `dynamicTools` empty;\n- approval policy on-request.\n\nEvery approval or elicitation the engine asks for is declined, and any\nother engine request is refused. Tools, project access and approvals are\nC21; workspace MCP servers are C22.\n\nOne prompt runs at a time: at most 32 KiB, as eight 8 KiB text items.\nAnswers stream into a memory-only transcript:\n- at most 200 entries and 1 MiB of text;\n- one answer at most 256 KiB; past that, the answer is stopped and marked\n  truncated;\n- any copy of the key in engine text is replaced.\n\nStop answer sends `turn/interrupt`. Nothing of a conversation is written\nto disk. Protected history and restore are C20.\n\n**Retries.** The pinned engine's provider retries a Gateway 5xx or\ntransport failure four more times within one attempt. It also reconnects\na dropped or failed stream up to five times, each with those retries\nagain. A failing Gateway was measured to receive 30 requests for one\nmessage. The bootstrap cannot change this. Instead, the host stops the turn\nat the engine's first reconnect notice (an `error` notification with\n`willRetry`), and the turn ends as failed. A failing Gateway therefore sees\nat most the five requests of one attempt, and no 4xx is retried. The\nengine's in-attempt retries remain an engine property. Removing them\nneeds an engine change. Live qualification is C25.\n\n**Not done here.** The following are not done in C19:\n- Gateway sign-in and refresh (C20);\n- protected history and restore (C20);\n- tools, proposals and project access (C21);\n- MCP (C22);\n- importing v1 history (C23);\n- signed packaging (C26).\n\n## D047 — Harness company sign-in, renewal and protected history\n\nDecided in C20 (implementer decision for the C20 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Credential choice.** A tenant may configure one company sign-in for the\nHarness: the Gateway workspace and runtime endpoint the token is for, and\nthe identity provider's issuer, public client ID and Gateway audience. The\noperator chooses, for that workspace, whether new conversations answer with\nthe workspace inference key (C18) or the company sign-in; signing in chooses\nthe sign-in. This follows v1's choice of a selected SSO session, otherwise\nthe stored key. Changing the choice, signing out, removing the\nconfiguration or the tenant retires the running conversation. The engine\nsends either credential as the Gateway key (`x-portkey-api-key`), as v1 did.\n\n**Sign-in.** OpenID Connect authorization code with PKCE (S256) through the\nsystem browser and a loopback redirect on `127.0.0.1` at an ephemeral port\n(RFC 8252 §7.3). The app is a public client.\n- Discovery must name exactly the configured issuer. It must support the\n  code flow and S256. Every endpoint (authorization, token, JWKS,\n  revocation) must be HTTPS on the issuer's own origin, so metadata never\n  grants a new destination (the Academy rule, D028). `offline_access` is\n  asked for when listed.\n- State and nonce are 256-bit random values. The verifier is 384 bits.\n- The listener lives for one attempt: 5 minutes, at most 32 connections,\n  3 s per connection, 8 KiB per request and 4 KiB per code.\n  - Only `GET /callback` with exactly one `Host` naming the listener is\n    considered.\n  - Only this attempt's `state` (compared in constant time) ends it.\n    Anything else is answered and ignored, so another local page can\n    neither complete nor cancel the attempt.\n  - An `iss` parameter must be the issuer. It is required when the provider\n    says it sends one (RFC 9207).\n- Replies are bounded at 128 KiB (JWKS 64 KiB, 32 keys), redirects are\n  refused, and 5xx or transport failures read as network failures.\n\n**Verification (stricter than v1, which did not check signatures).**\n- Both the ID token and the Gateway access token are JWTs, verified with\n  `jsonwebtoken` 11.1.0's verified `decode` against the issuer's JWKS. The\n  decode is never unchecked.\n- Only asymmetric algorithms are accepted: RS256/384/512, PS256/384/512,\n  ES256, ES384 and EdDSA. `none` and HMAC never verify.\n- The key must match the header: by `kid`, signature use, declared\n  algorithm, type and curve, and an RSA modulus of at least 2048 bits.\n- An unknown `kid` allows one bounded JWKS refresh per verification, so\n  each later key rotation is followed too (P06). Failing that, the token\n  is refused, never accepted unsigned.\n- ID token: issuer; the client as audience; the authorized party when\n  there are several audiences, or when present; this attempt's nonce; and\n  expiry and issued-at with 30 s skew.\n- Access token: issuer; the configured audience; `azp` or `client_id` when\n  present must be this client; expiry, not-before and issued-at with 30 s\n  skew. A `portkey_workspace` claim, when present, must be the chosen\n  workspace's slug.\n- The access token's `sub` is the grant's account. After a grant exists,\n  a token for another subject is `differentAccount` and the new refresh\n  token is revoked.\n- Opaque access tokens are refused (`opaqueToken`): no introspection\n  contract exists.\n- The provider is `aws_lc_rs`, not the `rust_crypto` that qualification.md\n  proposed. Reasons:\n  - aws-lc-rs 1.18.2 is already in the shipped graph (rustls-webpki).\n  - `rust_crypto` would add `rsa` 0.9, which carries an advisory with no\n    fixed release (RUSTSEC-2023-0071, a timing side channel in private-key\n    operations). Verification does not use private keys, but this choice\n    keeps the crate out.\n  - Default features are off: no PEM.\n- `openidconnect`/`oauth2` are not used, for D028's reasons: the authority\n  owns cancellation, flow supersession and revocation of a grant that\n  lands after cancel. Their HTTP integration targets another reqwest.\n  The flow is a small, fully bounded request set over the app's own client.\n\n**Grants and epochs.** Signing in creates a grant: a random UUID kept with\nthe configuration (nonsecret). The engine binding's credential is\n`derived_uuid(\"sign-in-grant\", grant)` with epoch 1. For the workspace key\nit stays C19's `derived_uuid(\"credential\", record)`.\n- Renewals keep the grant, so the binding, and with it the engine's exact\n  `replay.context == context` check, is unchanged.\n- Signing in again to the same account under a live grant (after a refused\n  renewal) also keeps it.\n- Signing out, removing the configuration or the tenant ends the grant. A\n  later sign-in is a new grant: it never revives an old conversation, whose\n  saved history can then be read and exported but not continued.\n- Changing the configuration requires signing out first.\n- The refresh token is a Keychain item (JSON, one per grant, new record\n  per rotation) under the C02 write-ahead cleanup ledger. The access token\n  is kept in memory only.\n- Both writes (sign-in and rotation) follow the confirmed write-ahead\n  contract (P06, C20 review F2). The record joins the ledger, the save is\n  confirmed durable, and only then is it written. It is marked in flight\n  until the save that settles it, under that save's lock, so cleanup and\n  reset wait for it. An unconfirmed ledger writes nothing. A\n  write that lands after its sign-in was cancelled, signed out or\n  superseded stays in the ledger and is deleted; the new token is revoked.\n- One renewal runs at a time per grant and runs to completion even if its\n  caller goes away. A rotated token is saved or revoked, never lost.\n- A sign-in again to the same account renews the live grant in place under\n  the same lock (P06, C20 review F3). It waits for a renewal under way, and\n  a renewal waits for it. A rotation commits only if the stored record is\n  still the one it started from. Exactly the current refresh record stays\n  owned, and the grant (with it the engine binding) is unchanged.\n\n**Renewal and restart.** Before each message, a sign-in conversation\ncompares the wall clock with the engine token's renewal time,\n`exp − min(120 s, lifetime/2)`.\n- Inside the margin, the message is held (phase `renewing`) while the\n  token is renewed. The engine accepts its credential only in the\n  bootstrap, so the running engine is then stopped and reaped. A new engine\n  is started with the new token and the same binding, and\n  `desktop/history/restore` restores the conversation's last export. Only\n  then is the message sent.\n- A refused or failed renewal sends nothing. The message is given back to\n  the box, and the conversation stays with `sessionExpired` (sign in\n  again).\n- A transient network failure gives the message back without expiring the\n  grant.\n- A conversation with turns but no export to restore cannot continue\n  (`restoreFailed`); it is never silently restarted without context.\n- An export matches the conversation only until its next turn ends. A\n  turn whose export is refused or unanswered leaves nothing to restore, so\n  the renewal fails as `restoreFailed` without starting an engine or\n  sending the message. An older export never stands in for newer turns\n  (P06, C20 review F1).\n- Sleep still ends conversations (C19), so expiry across sleep is met at\n  the next start or resume. The C20 conversation has no tools or effects,\n  so \"rollback to a pre-turn checkpoint\" has nothing to roll back: a\n  failed turn stays shown as failed, and nothing is resent. Tool and\n  effect recovery, and WorkspaceWrite re-confirmation on restart, are C21.\n\n**Protected history.** After every turn (completed, stopped, cut or\nfailed) the host asks the idle engine for `desktop/history/read`. A turn\ncut short when the engine itself is stopped is saved with the last export,\nor without one when none exists (`notRestorable`: read and exported, never\ncontinued), and its unfinished entries `interrupted` (D049, P06).\nA stop that comes after a turn finished, while its export is still under\nway, waits for it for up to 3 s. The turn is then saved with its export\nand stays continuable. A finished turn's transcript counts as unsaved\nuntil a save succeeds. If its export does not come, is refused or is\nmalformed, or never answers within its 15 s bound, a stop saves the\ntranscript without a restore point: read-only, and never paired with an\nolder export (P06 phase review F1, R2-F2). Once a stop has begun, nothing\noperator-sent is released, after any await: not a message waiting for an\nexport, not one still queued, and not a renewal's (no engine is started\nfor it). Each such message is kept in the transcript as `failed`, saved\nwith it (the last export stays the restore point for the turns it holds),\nand never sent or replayed later; so is a renewal's message whose new\nengine never runs (its home, launch or pipes fail, or the old home cannot\nbe removed), saved with the last export (P06 phase review R4-F1). A\nrenewal a stop overtakes while its\nsign-in is renewed, or while changes wait to be allowed again, gives its\nmessage back to the box instead (P06 phase review R2-F1, R3-F1, R3-F2).\nMessages wait for the read, which is bounded by the 15 s request timeout. The\nconversation is saved as one sealed record:\n- the transcript;\n- the binding it ran under;\n- the engine's replay and context exactly as exported.\n\nStorage:\n- `<data>/harness-history/<tenant digest>/<scope digest>/<id>.sealed`, one\n  scope per tenant and Gateway workspace.\n- XChaCha20-Poly1305 (`chacha20poly1305` 0.10.1, D031; the DESIGN's 0.11.0\n  is not adopted), with a fresh 24-byte nonce per write.\n- A random 32-byte key per scope in the Keychain, at a record derived from\n  the scope, made on first save.\n- Associated data binds the schema, the scope and the record ID.\n- Files are 0600 in 0700 directories and replaced atomically.\n- Scopes are noted in the state document before their first write, so\n  removing a tenant removes its keys (cleanup ledger) and its directory.\n- Each save is admitted under the authority lock (storage ready, tenant\n  present, the scope's note confirmed durable) and counted until it\n  settles. An unconfirmed note writes nothing (C20 review R2). Its scope key is kept in flight\n  meanwhile (P06, C20 review F4), and saves are refused once reset or\n  tenant removal begins. Tenant removal deletes the directory only after\n  that tenant's saves settle, and cleanup deletes the key after them.\n  Reset waits for every save, then purges the history folder with the\n  Keychain namespace; a failure is reported as a reset failure. A launch\n  removes history folders of tenants that no longer exist.\n- Locked, denied or missing storage never falls back to plaintext. The\n  conversation goes on unsaved and says why.\n\nLimits:\n- 16 conversations per workspace. A seventeenth is refused (`full`),\n  never made room for by deleting.\n- 8 MiB per record. The engine's replay is at most 4 MiB, and the\n  transcript bounds are C19's.\n- A record that does not open is listed as unreadable, can be deleted, and\n  is never rewritten.\n\nOperations:\n- **Open** reads one record.\n- **Export** writes the transcript only (Markdown, text or JSON), never the\n  replay or context, through the native save sheet.\n- **Delete** asks for confirmation (an unreadable record too: its key may\n  be missing only for now) and is refused for the running\n  conversation.\n- **Continue** starts a new engine and restores the record. It is offered\n  only when the record's binding equals the binding a new conversation\n  would have now (tenant, workspace, credential, epoch, endpoint and\n  credential choice). Otherwise the record is read-only and says why. The\n  engine checks the context again. The continued conversation keeps\n  updating the same record.\n- **Offline (T23).** With no workspace chosen, as when AI Gateway cannot\n  be read, the tenant's last chosen workspace's history is listed from\n  this Mac (`local`). It can be opened, exported and deleted but not\n  continued (`workspaceUnavailable`). A launch or reconnect never restarts\n  or resubmits a conversation; continuing is the operator's explicit\n  choice from history.\n- Imported v1 history is C23 and stays inspect-only.\n\n**Out of scope here:** tools, approvals, project access and WorkspaceWrite\n(C21); MCP and its token rotation (C22); v1 history import (C23); live\nidentity-provider and Gateway qualification (C25).\n\n## D048 — Harness desktop tools, project folder, approvals and proposals\n\nDecided in C21 (implementer decision for the C21 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Desktop tools.** The engine is given one dynamic-tool namespace,\n`airs_desktop`, and nothing else of the desktop. Its tools are typed:\n- `inventory`, `detail`: the Runtime Security inventories C08 reads.\n  The DLP catalog is not offered.\n- `session`, `search`: C11's investigation reads. Session content is\n  already stripped by the management layer.\n- `redTeam`, `supplyChain`: C13's observation reads.\n- `scan`: one C03 scan with a profile named by the model, through the\n  tenant's own scan key.\n- `cases`: the C12 library's names and expectations.\n- `proposeChange`: stages a change for the operator to review (below).\n\nEvery call goes through the same admission as the operator's own reads:\n`admit_management`, `admit_scan` and the library. The call runs under the\nconversation's context and is refused once that context is stale. The\ndesktop holds the credentials. Results are JSON:\n- JSON carried as text is opened first: at most 4 layers deep and 4 MiB.\n  Text still encoded past those bounds is replaced by a note, never\n  passed on unchecked (P06, C21 reviews F2 and R2-F2);\n- field names that carry credentials are removed at any depth (14\n  names, case- and dash-insensitive);\n- the conversation's key is redacted;\n- a longer result becomes `{\"cut\": note, \"partial\": text}`, shortened\n  until the whole envelope, escaping included, fits 24 KiB (P06, C21\n  review R2-F4).\n\nA call is parsed into a closed set:\n- `deny_unknown_fields`;\n- arguments of at most 40 KiB, pages of at most 50 and scan text of at\n  most 16 KiB;\n- any credential-like argument name is refused.\n\nThe desktop runs a call only when it belongs to the turn being answered,\nand only once per call ID; a repeat is a protocol failure. Anything else\nis answered \"This tool or its arguments are unavailable.\" and nothing\nruns. At most 8 calls or approvals wait at a time. A call has 60 s, and\nthe engine's pending tool calls are dropped with the turn (interrupt or\nend). Each call carries its run and turn lease. When the turn ends, the\ncall is cancelled, and so is the runtime operation it admitted (P06, C21\nreviews F1 and R2-F1): queued work settles NotSent and sends nothing;\nwork already dispatched keeps its own outcome; no result is sent late. This follows C19's rule that a reply to a request the engine forgot\nis fatal.\n\n**Proposals, never Apply.** `proposeChange` accepts Runtime and DLP kinds\nonly, with create, update or delete. Gateway changes need the operator's\nown workspace review (C17). Before staging, the proposal passes the same\nstatic checks a review makes, through the tenant's management client\n(`changes::normalize`). Nothing is read or sent at that point.\n- A conversation holds at most 8 proposals, numbered `P-1`… The model is\n  told that nothing was applied.\n- Review opens the proposal with the operator's existing\n  `review_change`. It is refused (`Busy`) while any change review is\n  open, so the operator's own draft or review is never replaced.\n- Administration shows the opened review with the proposal's exact\n  draft, so Apply sends what was reviewed. Only the operator's\n  `RequestApply` and the native confirmation (C09) write.\n- A proposal reads `inReview` only while its review is the open one, then\n  `closed`. Dismissed proposals are hidden.\n\n**Project folder and access.** The operator chooses one folder per tenant\nin the native \"Choose project folder\" sheet. The folder is stored as\nnonsecret state with a write flag that is off by default. These folders\nare refused:\n- the home folder itself, and `.ssh`, `.aws`, `.gnupg`, `.config`, `.kube`,\n  `.docker`, `Library`, `.azure` and `.netrc` under it;\n- `/System`, `/usr`, `/bin`, `/sbin`, `/private/etc`, `/private/var/db`,\n  `/private/var/root`, `/Library`, `/Applications`, `/dev` and `/cores`;\n- this app's data root, and any folder containing it.\n\nThe folder and its access are part of the engine binding:\n- The bootstrap carries `projectPath` and `localAccess`\n  (`readOnly`/`workspaceWrite`).\n- The thread carries `cwd` and the `read-only` or `workspace-write`\n  sandbox.\n- Without a folder, the thread has no local environment.\n\nChanging the folder or its access retires the running conversation. A\nsaved conversation whose folder or access differs reads `otherProject` and\ncannot be continued: a new conversation is needed. Restoring a compatible\none restores no approval, and no command is run again.\n\n**Approvals.** The engine's command and file-change requests wait for the\noperator under the desktop's own token, 12 random bytes. A token is valid\nonly for that run and turn and is cleared at interrupt and at turn end, so\na stale or repeated decision is refused. A request is declined at once,\nwithout asking, when any of these holds:\n- there is no folder;\n- it is a file change while changes are off;\n- it asks for added permissions, network access or another root;\n- it belongs to another turn;\n- what it would do cannot be shown whole: a command longer than 8 KiB (or\n  none), or a file change whose every file is not named whole (none\n  named, a change without a path, more than 64 files or a path past\n  1 KiB) (P06, C21 reviews F3 and R2-F3: never shown cut or partial);\n- 8 decisions are already waiting.\n\nThe pinned engine's own desktop sandbox enforces the rest. The negative\nprobes are recorded:\n- one process, with exec replacement only, so children and pipelines fail\n  with \"fork: Operation not permitted\";\n- no network;\n- reads outside the folder fail with \"Operation not permitted\";\n- writes only inside the folder, and only with `workspaceWrite`.\n\nThe engine's own patch writer (`apply_patch`) names its files only with\nthe change item, which may reach the desktop before or after the approval\nrequest. Asked first, the request is declined at once; asked after, the\napproval shows the named files whole. Approved, the patch still fails to\nwrite in this build: it runs in the engine's narrower helper sandbox (the\nengine is not changed). Allowed shell commands are what change the folder.\nBoth orders are exercised (harness_tools, native C21-N04).\n\n**Renewal with changes allowed.** When a company sign-in renewal\n(D047) would restart an engine that may change the folder, the message is\nheld (`confirmWrite`). Allow renews, restarts and restores with the same\naccess, then sends. Not now gives the message back, and nothing is\nrestarted, restored or sent.\n\n**Out of scope here:** MCP servers and their token rotation (C22);\nimported history (C23); live management, Gateway and model qualification\n(C25).\n\n## D049 — Workspace MCP servers for the Harness: catalog, desktop OAuth, grants and approval\n\nDecided in C22 (implementer decision for the C22 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Catalog.** The chosen Gateway workspace's MCP servers are read through\nthe tenant's management client, as one more scoped Gateway read:\n- `GET /mcp-servers?workspace_id`, zero-based pages of 100, at most five.\n- Only the workspace's own servers are kept.\n- A server's URL must be HTTPS with a host and no credentials, query or\n  fragment. It is kept as the `url` crate canonicalizes it, which is what\n  the engine stores.\n- Repeated IDs are dropped, and the upstream (`mcp_integration_url`) is\n  never kept.\n\nA server's revision is SHA-256 over a versioned domain and the\nlength-prefixed ID, slug, URL, status and update time. Its engine namespace\nis derived from its slug, as v1 derived it. A shared or reserved name gets a\nsuffix from the server's ID hash, wide enough to tell the group apart, so\nthe result does not depend on catalog order. An ID the engine cannot take\nis shown and never granted.\n\n**Sign-in is the desktop's, never the engine's.**\n- The server must answer an unauthenticated `initialize` with `401` and a\n  `resource_metadata` challenge on its own origin.\n- The protected-resource metadata (RFC 9728) must name exactly the URL.\n- Its authorization server must be on the same HTTPS origin, and that\n  server's metadata (RFC 8414) must name itself. It must offer the code\n  flow with S256 and a public client (`none`) and client registration, and\n  keep every endpoint on that origin.\n- A client is registered for each attempt (RFC 7591).\n- The browser goes to authorize with:\n  - PKCE S256 and a 256-bit `state`;\n  - `resource` set to the URL (RFC 8707);\n  - the shared one-attempt `127.0.0.1` loopback (D047);\n  - only `mcp:servers:read`, `mcp:tools:list` and `mcp:tools:call`, as\n    offered (never a wildcard).\n- The code is exchanged, and later refreshed, with `resource`.\n- Replies are at most 64 KiB and redirects are refused.\n- An access token must be a bearer of 16-16384 visible ASCII bytes, as the\n  engine requires.\n- These requests are written by hand over the app's own client, like\n  D047's: `oauth2`/`openidconnect` do not cover RFC 9728/8414/7591 discovery\n  and registration, and D028's reasons hold.\n\n**Grants.** A sign-in is a grant: a new UUID bound to the server's URL and\nrevision at that moment. Its tokens, issue time and expiry are a Keychain\nitem.\n- Writing it, and each rotation, follows the full credential-store\n  contract. The record joins the cleanup ledger, confirmed durable, and is\n  reserved as in flight before it is written. It leaves the ledger only in\n  the save that names it, so a stale or failed write is never left\n  untracked.\n- The in-flight reservation ends under the same lock as that settlement\n  (P06, C22 review F4), so cleanup can never delete a record between its\n  write and the save that names it. Company sign-in (D047) uses the same\n  functions.\n- A refresh renews inside `min(120 s, lifetime/2)` and rotates the item.\n  One renewal runs at a time per grant, and it completes even if its caller\n  goes away.\n- Signing out, removing the tenant or resetting deletes the item through\n  the ledger, then revokes both tokens at the server (best effort).\n- Signing out of a server also ends a sign-in to it that is under way\n  (P06, C22 review F3). A late callback or token reply then installs\n  nothing, and its tokens are revoked.\n\n**A conversation's scope** is fixed at its start:\n- the servers turned on (at most eight per workspace) that are in the\n  loaded catalog, in catalog order;\n- each with its grant only if that grant was made for the server's current\n  URL and revision.\n\nWith any server on, the catalog must have been read successfully, with no\nread under way, or nothing starts (`Busy`). A failed or pending read keeps\nthe last catalog for display only (P06, C22 review F2). Until a read\nsucceeds:\n- no conversation starts or continues from history (`otherMcp`);\n- a running conversation sends nothing (its message stays in the box);\n- every MCP approval is declined natively.\n\nA changed revision or URL in the last catalog read still retires the\nconversation.\nThe bootstrap carries `mcpServers` in scope order. Each server gets its\ntoken, or the 32-byte placeholder `airs-desktop-mcp-sign-in-expired` when\nthere is no current sign-in, so the scope stays the same and that server\nfails to start while inference goes on. The receipt must echo exactly the\nscope's `{name, serverId}` in order. Tokens that do not fit the 64 KiB frame\nfail the start with `mcpTooLarge` and are never truncated.\n\n**What retires a conversation.** Any of these retires a conversation whose\nscope holds the server:\n- signing out;\n- signing in again (a new grant, even for the same URL);\n- turning the server off;\n- a new catalog revision or URL;\n- removing the tenant.\n\nAn expired sign-in does not. Grants and revisions are not in the engine's\ncontext, so the desktop checks them, as D048 checks the folder.\n\n**Approval.** An elicitation from the engine is held for the operator only\nwhen all of these hold:\n- it is form mode, with `codex_approval_kind: mcp_tool_call`;\n- its `airs_desktop_item_id` names an `mcpToolCall` the engine started in\n  this turn, on the same server, for a server in the scope;\n- that call has not been asked before;\n- the server holds a current token;\n- fewer than eight decisions are waiting.\n\nThe card shows the server, the tool and the arguments of the call as the\nengine started it, never the request's own `_meta`. Anything else is\ndeclined at once with a transcript notice; the engine's reserved-key\nforgeries never arrive. The operator's Approve once or Decline is sent as\nexactly `{\"action\":\"accept\"}` or `{\"action\":\"decline\"}`. When an approval\nis decided, the desktop checks again that the server's grant is the scope's\nand is unexpired, both by failure and by the token's own expiry against\nthe clock (P06, C22 review F1). A call held across expiry, sleep\nincluded, or first offered after it, is declined whatever the operator\nchose. Pending\napprovals end with the turn or an interrupt, and no late reply is sent.\nCalls, results and errors are bounded, and every token is redacted.\n\n**Rotation.** Before a message, the scope's tokens are renewed when one is\ninside its margin. Only if what the engine holds changed (a fingerprint\nover the server IDs and tokens) is it restarted, restoring the last export\nand then sending. An MCP-only rotation keeps the conversation's confirmed\naccess to its folder (D048). When the Gateway credential is renewed too,\none restart does both and D048's re-confirmation applies.\n\n**History.** A saved conversation records its scope. One whose scope\ndiffers from what a new conversation would get now, or cannot be checked\nbecause the catalog is not loaded, reads `otherMcp` and cannot be\ncontinued.\n\n**Offline and reconnecting (T23).** A turn that a stop (sleep, quit or\nending the conversation) cuts short while it is open is saved as it\nstood. This includes a turn whose call has settled while it waits for the\nmodel's next answer (C22 review R3-F1). Its unfinished\nentries read `interrupted`, so an approved call that may have run at the\nserver is recorded with an unknown outcome. The record keeps the last\nexport, which does not hold that turn, so continuing restores only what\nthe engine had (P06, C22 review G1). With no export (the first turn, or\nafter one was refused) it is saved without a restore point. It reads\n`notRestorable` and can be read and exported but never continued, so no\ncontext is invented (C22 review R2-F1). Pending MCP approvals and calls live\nonly with the conversation in memory. A relaunch, offline or not, never\nrestores, re-asks or resubmits them. Servers' enablement and sign-ins are\nkept (the state document and the Keychain), and nothing is called until\nthe operator starts a conversation again (P06,\n`a_pending_mcp_call_never_survives_going_offline_or_runs_on_reconnect`).\n\n**Fixtures.** The integration tests and the native driver serve the MCP\nplane over loopback HTTPS that the engine trusts through the app's own\nroots (`CODEX_CA_CERTIFICATE`). The engine's `--allow-http-loopback` is\nnever passed.\n\n**Out of scope here:** v1 history and MCP-state import (C23); live\nGateway MCP sign-in, denial, refresh and revocation qualification (C25).\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'endHarness|requestApply|reviewHarnessProposal|Principal::Harness|harness_allowed|allowed_for|ConfirmHarness' crates/desktop-core/src/authority.rs | head -85\n sed -n '1405,1445p' packages/desktop-contracts/src/client.ts\n sed -n '1560,1596p' packages/desktop-contracts/src/client.ts\n sed -n '3090,3190p' packages/desktop-contracts/src/client.ts\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'endHarness|requestApply|reviewHarnessProposal|Principal::Harness|harness_allowed|allowed_for|ConfirmHarness' crates/desktop-core/src/authority.rs | head -85\n sed -n '1405,1445p' packages/desktop-contracts/src/client.ts\n sed -n '1560,1596p' packages/desktop-contracts/src/client.ts\n sed -n '3090,3190p' packages/desktop-contracts/src/client.ts\"","aggregated_output":"411:    ConfirmHarnessWrite {\n480:    SendHarness {\n822:    RequestApply => \"requestApply\",\n847:    ReviewHarnessProposal => \"reviewHarnessProposal\",\n849:    ConfirmHarnessWrite => \"confirmHarnessWrite\",\n861:    SendHarness => \"sendHarness\",\n863:    EndHarness => \"endHarness\",\n2741:    ConfirmHarness {\n4397:                effects.push(Effect::ConfirmHarness {\n4427:        Intent::ConfirmHarnessWrite {\n4568:        Intent::SendHarness {\n5361:            Effect::ConfirmHarness {\n\n/** The project the Harness works in, and whether it may change it (C21). */\nfunction isHarnessProjectView(value: unknown): boolean {\n    return (\n        isRecord(value) &&\n        typeof value.path === 'string' &&\n        value.path.startsWith('/') &&\n        value.path.length <= 1024 &&\n        typeof value.write === 'boolean' &&\n        Object.keys(value).length === 2\n    )\n}\n\nfunction bounded(max: number) {\n    return (v: unknown) => typeof v === 'string' && utf8Bytes(v) <= max + 3\n}\n\n/** An engine request for the operator's approval (C21). */\nfunction isHarnessApproval(value: unknown): boolean {\n    return (\n        isRecord(value) &&\n        typeof value.id === 'string' &&\n        APPROVAL_ID.test(value.id) &&\n        oneOf(['command', 'fileChange', 'mcpTool', 'desktopRead'], value.kind) &&\n        // C32: what allowing it lets the engine do, as the core established it\n        // (absent only before C32, and then treated as may-change).\n        optional((e) => oneOf(['readOnly', 'mayChange'], e), value.effect ?? null) &&\n        // A desktop read only reads, and says so; a file change or a remote tool may change things.\n        (value.kind !== 'desktopRead' || value.effect === 'readOnly') &&\n        ((value.kind !== 'fileChange' && value.kind !== 'mcpTool') || value.effect !== 'readOnly') &&\n        // Shown whole: never cut (C21 F3).\n        optional((v) => typeof v === 'string' && utf8Bytes(v) <= MAX_APPROVAL_BYTES, value.command) &&\n        optional(bounded(1024), value.cwd) &&\n        optional(bounded(1024), value.reason) &&\n        Array.isArray(value.files) &&\n        value.files.length <= 64 &&\n        value.files.every((f) => typeof f === 'string' && f.length > 0 && utf8Bytes(f) <= 1024) &&\n        // Only a file change names files, and it names every one whole\n        // (C21 review R2-F3).\n        (value.kind === 'fileChange' ? value.files.length > 0 : value.files.length === 0) &&\n        optional((v) => typeof v === 'string' && MCP_NAME.test(v), value.server) &&\n}\n\nfunction isSavedConversation(value: unknown): boolean {\n    return (\n        isRecord(value) &&\n        typeof value.id === 'string' &&\n        UUID.test(value.id) &&\n        typeof value.title === 'string' &&\n        [...value.title].length <= MAX_TITLE_CHARS &&\n        isCount(value.updated) &&\n        isCount(value.entries) &&\n        (value.entries as number) <= MAX_HARNESS_ENTRIES &&\n        oneOf(CREDENTIAL_SOURCES, value.source)\n    )\n}\n\n/** Sends a conversation took by the presentation's ID, with the message each became (C32): bounded, each ID once. */\nconst SUBMISSION = /^[0-9a-f]{32}$/\nconst MAX_SUBMISSIONS = 64\nfunction isTakenSubmissions(value: unknown): boolean {\n    return (\n        Array.isArray(value) &&\n        value.length <= MAX_SUBMISSIONS &&\n        value.every(\n            (t) => isRecord(t) && typeof t.submission === 'string' && SUBMISSION.test(t.submission) && typeof t.entry === 'string' && /^u-[0-9]{1,20}$/.test(t.entry) && oneOf(['pending', 'delivered', 'returned'], t.state)\n        ) &&\n        new Set(value.map((t) => (t as { submission: string }).submission)).size === value.length\n    )\n}\n\n/** A conversation's opening message, whole (C32): absent when not known, never empty. */\nconst isOpening = (value: unknown): boolean => typeof value === 'string' && value.length > 0 && utf8Bytes(value) <= MAX_TRANSCRIPT_BYTES\n\nfunction isOpenedConversation(value: unknown): boolean {\n    if (!isRecord(value)) return false\n    const entries = value.entries\n    return (\n            throw error\n        }\n        if (isRecord(response) && response.kind === 'management') return parseManagementResult(response.result)\n        if (isRecord(response) && response.kind === 'refusal') throw new RefusedError(asRefusal(response.refusal))\n        throw new ContractViolation('management observation')\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Waits for the single result of an admitted API Client request. Not an\n     * admission: it may be called concurrently with dispatches.\n     */\n    async observeWorkbench(operation: string): Promise<WorkbenchResult> {\n        if (!OPERATION_ID.test(operation)) throw new ContractViolation('operation reference')\n        let response: unknown\n        try {\n            response = await this.#transport.observe(\n                encode({ protocol: PROTOCOL, scope: { kind: 'workbench', operation } })\n            )\n        } catch (error) {\n            if (typeof error === 'string' && (REFUSALS as readonly string[]).includes(error)) {\n                throw new RefusedError(error as Refusal)\n            }\n            throw error\n        }\n        if (isRecord(response) && response.kind === 'workbench') return parseWorkbenchResult(response.result)\n        if (isRecord(response) && response.kind === 'refusal') throw new RefusedError(asRefusal(response.refusal))\n        throw new ContractViolation('workbench observation')\n    }\n\n    async #register(): Promise<Session> {\n        const response = await this.#transport.observe(\n            encode({ protocol: PROTOCOL, scope: { kind: 'deliverySession' } })\n        )\n        if (isRecord(response) && response.kind === 'deliverySession' && !isDecimalU64(response.nextSequence)) {\n            throw new ContractViolation('delivery session sequence')\n        }\n        if (isRecord(response) && response.kind === 'refusal') {\n            throw new RefusedError(asRefusal(response.refusal))\n        }\n        if (\n            !isRecord(response) ||\n            response.kind !== 'deliverySession' ||\n            typeof response.session !== 'string' ||\n            response.session.length === 0\n        ) {\n            throw new ContractViolation('delivery session')\n        }\n        return { nonce: response.session, next: asSequence(response.nextSequence) }\n    }\n\n    async #deliver(intent: Intent): Promise<DispatchResponse> {\n        if (this.#session === null || this.#session.next > U64_MAX) {\n            this.#session = await this.#register()\n        }\n        const session = this.#session\n        const bytes = encode({\n            protocol: PROTOCOL,\n            session: session.nonce,\n            sequence: session.next.toString(),\n            intent,\n        })\n        for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= this.#resends; attempt += 1) {\n            let raw: unknown\n            try {\n                raw = await this.#transport.dispatch(bytes)\n            } catch {\n                continue\n            }\n            // A result arrived: the sequence is consumed whatever it says.\n            session.next += 1n\n            return parseDispatchResponse(raw)\n        }\n        // Ambiguous delivery: never reuse this sequence for other input.\n        this.#session = null\n        throw new DeliveryOutcomeUnknown(intent.kind)\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Observes the shell. On native resync (overflow or channel end) it\n     * observes again and delivers a fresh snapshot; stale revisions are\n     * ignored. Every replaced, failed or stopped observation is closed on\n     * both sides: the JavaScript callback is removed and the native task is\n     * told to stop. Stopping observation never cancels native work.\n     */\n    async observeShell(observer: ShellObserver): Promise<() => void> {\n        let stopped = false\n        let latest = -1n\n        let channel: { token: string; close(): void } | null = null\n\n        const retire = (current: { token: string; close(): void } | null) => {\n            if (!current) return\n            current.close()\n            void this.#transport\n                .observe(encode({ protocol: PROTOCOL, scope: { kind: 'closeShell', updates: current.token } }))\n                .catch(() => undefined)\n        }\n\n        const deliver = (snapshot: ShellSnapshot) => {\n            const revision = BigInt(snapshot.revision)\n            if (revision <= latest) return\n            latest = revision\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs/run-checks.sh\n python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; r=json.load(open(\\\"docs/architecture/checks.json\\\")); print(\\\"CHECK manifest keys\\\",list(r)); print(\\\"C32\\\",json.dumps(r.get(\\\"chunks\\\",{}).get(\\\"C32\\\"),indent=2)); print(\\\"INPUT LOCK\\\",Path(\\\"docs/architecture/inputs.lock.json\\\").read_text()[:4400]); n=json.load(open(\\\"docs/architecture/native-checks.json\\\")); print(\\\"C32 native\\\",json.dumps(n[\\\"chunks\\\"][\\\"C32\\\"],indent=2))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs/run-checks.sh\n python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; r=json.load(open(\\\"docs/architecture/checks.json\\\")); print(\\\"CHECK manifest keys\\\",list(r)); print(\\\"C32\\\",json.dumps(r.get(\\\"chunks\\\",{}).get(\\\"C32\\\"),indent=2)); print(\\\"INPUT LOCK\\\",Path(\\\"docs/architecture/inputs.lock.json\\\").read_text()[:4400]); n=json.load(open(\\\"docs/architecture/native-checks.json\\\")); print(\\\"C32 native\\\",json.dumps(n[\\\"chunks\\\"][\\\"C32\\\"],indent=2))'\"","aggregated_output":"#!/bin/bash\n# P09 check runs with a source-bound record. Usage: run-checks.sh <attempt>\n# Runs on the approved toolchain (rustup 1.99.0, node 24.21.0, repo pnpm).\nset -o pipefail\nATTEMPT=${1:-attempt-01}\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../../../../..\"\nexport PATH=\"$PWD/.tools/bin:$HOME/.cargo/bin:$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.21.0/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\"\nunset RUSTFLAGS CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS CARGO_TARGET_DIR\n# Timing-sensitive suites (4 s \"eventually\" waits, engine bootstraps) flake when ten test threads\n# per binary share this Mac with the owner's browser and a second session; four threads per\n# binary keep the same tests and assertions, only less contention. Recorded in the run record.\nexport RUST_TEST_THREADS=4\nR=docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs\nE=$R/$ATTEMPT\nif [ -e \"$E\" ] || [ -e \"$R/run-$ATTEMPT.json\" ]; then\n  echo \"Refusing to overwrite retained P09 check evidence: $ATTEMPT; choose a fresh attempt.\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\nmkdir -p \"$E\"\n# Flake logs are same-input retries of this run only: earlier runs' logs are removed first.\nrm -f $E/core-fixtures.flake-*.log $E/host.flake-*.log\nBEFORE=$($R/hash-inputs.sh \"$PWD\" $E/inputs-before.sha256) || { echo \"input inventory failed; run invalid\"; exit 2; }\n[ -n \"$BEFORE\" ] || { echo \"input inventory produced no digest; run invalid\"; exit 2; }\n# Configuration and build flags the controller fingerprints: itemised and hashed together.\nBUILD_FLAGS=$(shasum -a 256 docs/architecture/checks.json docs/architecture/native-checks.json docs/architecture/toolchain.json docs/architecture/budgets.json apps/desktop/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json apps/desktop/src-tauri/tauri.fixture.conf.json apps/desktop/src-tauri/capabilities/*.json rust-toolchain.toml 2>/dev/null | shasum -a 256 | cut -d' ' -f1)\nexport BUILD_FLAGS\nrun() { local name=$1; shift; local log=$E/$name.log; echo \"\\$ $*\" > \"$log\"; \"$@\" >> \"$log\" 2>&1; local rc=$?; echo \"EXIT $rc\" >> \"$log\"; echo \"$name exit=$rc\"; echo $rc > $E/$name.exit; }\nrun rustfmt cargo fmt --all -- --check\nrun clippy cargo clippy --locked -p desktop-core -p desktop-host -p xtask --features test-fixtures --all-targets -- -D warnings\nrun clippy-default cargo clippy --locked --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\nrun notices cargo run --locked -q -p xtask -- notices --check\nrun bindings cargo run --locked -q -p xtask -- bindings --check\nrun contracts-typecheck pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop-contracts exec tsc --noEmit\nrun desktop-typecheck pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec tsc --noEmit\nrun vitest pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec vitest run --config vitest.config.ts\nrun vitest-truth pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec vitest run --config vitest.truth.config.ts\nrun core-lib cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --lib\nrun core-fixtures cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --features test-fixtures\n# A timing-sensitive test (Harness-engine turns, or the transport's connection-release\n# check) can flake under the full-suite load: the exact same\n# command is retried on the same frozen inputs, every failed run kept as evidence.\nfor try in 1 2; do\n  if [ \"$(cat $E/core-fixtures.exit)\" != \"0\" ]; then\n    cp $E/core-fixtures.log $E/core-fixtures.flake-$try.log\n    run core-fixtures cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --features test-fixtures\n  fi\ndone\n# The chunk's registered fixture command (docs/architecture/checks.json P09), exactly as the manifest runs it.\nrun fixture-command cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --features test-fixtures --test ui_v2_closure --test ui_v2_shell_port --test ui_v2_home --test ui_v2_inbox_port --test ui_v2_harness_port --test ui_v2_admin --test ui_v2_api --test ui_v2_academy --test ui_v2_wizard\nrun host cargo test --locked -p desktop-host --features test-fixtures\n# The host's Academy device-flow tests (browser_callback.rs, outside this chunk) wait 5 s for a\n# state and flake under load: the same command is retried on the same frozen inputs, every\n# failed run kept as evidence.\nfor try in 1 2; do\n  if [ \"$(cat $E/host.exit)\" != \"0\" ]; then\n    cp $E/host.log $E/host.flake-$try.log\n    run host cargo test --locked -p desktop-host --features test-fixtures\n  fi\ndone\nrun xtask-lib cargo test --locked -p xtask --lib\nrun shell-controller cargo test --locked -p xtask --features test-fixtures --test shell_controller\nrun desktop-build pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop build\nrun l19-offline cargo test --locked -p xtask --features test-fixtures --test controlled_live --test live_run\n# Offline unittest only: retained/synthetic AX settlement and tempfile budget\n# ledgers. Imports pure helpers; never calls the live driver's guarded main(),\n# resolves credentials, launches the qualification app, or sends a product call.\nrun l19-native-driver python3 -I tools/native-acceptance/test_live_c29_l19.py\nAFTER=$($R/hash-inputs.sh \"$PWD\" $E/inputs-after.sha256) || { echo \"input inventory failed after the runs; run invalid\"; exit 2; }\n[ -n \"$AFTER\" ] || { echo \"input inventory produced no digest after the runs; run invalid\"; exit 2; }\npython3 -I - \"$E\" \"$BEFORE\" \"$AFTER\" \"$ATTEMPT\" <<'PY'\nimport json,sys,subprocess,os,time,hashlib\nE,b,a,attempt=sys.argv[1:5]\ndef ver(c):\n    try: return subprocess.check_output(c,text=True,stderr=subprocess.STDOUT).strip()\n    except Exception as e: return str(e)\nsha=lambda p: hashlib.sha256(open(p,\"rb\").read()).hexdigest()\nnames=[\"rustfmt\",\"clippy\",\"clippy-default\",\"notices\",\"bindings\",\"contracts-typecheck\",\"desktop-typecheck\",\"vitest\",\"vitest-truth\",\"core-lib\",\"core-fixtures\",\"fixture-command\",\"host\",\"xtask-lib\",\"shell-controller\",\"desktop-build\",\"l19-offline\",\"l19-native-driver\"]\nrec={\"subject\":\"P09\",\"attempt\":attempt,\"time\":time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z\"),\"cwd\":os.getcwd(),\n \"toolchain\":{\"rustc\":ver([\"rustc\",\"-V\"]),\"cargo\":ver([\"cargo\",\"-V\"]),\"node\":ver([\"node\",\"-v\"]),\"pnpm\":ver([\"pnpm\",\"-v\"]),\"rustup_active\":ver([\"rustup\",\"show\",\"active-toolchain\"])},\n \"environment\":{\"PATH\":os.environ[\"PATH\"],\"RUSTFLAGS\":os.environ.get(\"RUSTFLAGS\"),\"CARGO_TARGET_DIR\":os.environ.get(\"CARGO_TARGET_DIR\"),\"RUST_TEST_THREADS\":os.environ.get(\"RUST_TEST_THREADS\")},\n \"build_flags\":{\"digest\":os.environ.get(\"BUILD_FLAGS\"),\"covers\":\"checks.json, native-checks.json, toolchain.json, budgets.json, tauri.conf.json, tauri.fixture.conf.json, src-tauri/capabilities/*.json, rust-toolchain.toml\"},\"inputs\":{\"procedure\":f\"{E}/../hash-inputs.sh\",\"procedure_sha256\":sha(f\"{E}/../hash-inputs.sh\"),\"before_inventory_sha256\":sha(f\"{E}/inputs-before.sha256\"),\"before_digest\":b,\"after_inventory_sha256\":sha(f\"{E}/inputs-after.sha256\"),\"after_digest\":a,\"unchanged\":a==b,\"members\":sum(1 for _ in open(f\"{E}/inputs-before.sha256\"))},\n \"runs\":{}}\nfor n in names:\n    log=f\"{E}/{n}.log\"\n    rec[\"runs\"][n]={\"exit\":int(open(f\"{E}/{n}.exit\").read().strip()),\"log\":log,\"log_sha256\":sha(log),\"summary\":[l.strip() for l in open(log,errors=\"replace\") if l.startswith(\"test result\") or \"Test Files\" in l or \"Tests  \" in l or l.startswith(\"EXIT\")][-4:]}\njson.dump(rec,open(f\"{E}/../run-{attempt}.json\",\"w\"),indent=2)\nprint(json.dumps({k:v[\"exit\"] for k,v in rec[\"runs\"].items()}),\"inputs unchanged:\",a==b,\"members:\",rec[\"inputs\"][\"members\"])\nPY\nCHECK manifest keys ['schema_version', 'status', 'target', 'fixture_only_features', 'setup_commands', 'common_commands', 'controller', 'shared_source_commands', 'artifact_build', 'chunks', 'phases', 'phase_native_prepare', 'invariants', 'requested_production_features', 'effective_features', 'controller_commands', 'reviewer', 'shared_lifecycle_contract', 'shared_source_required_chunks', 'shared_source_requirement', 'environment_contract']\nC32 {\n  \"rust_target\": \"ui_v2_harness\",\n  \"fixture_command\": [\n    \"cargo\",\n    \"test\",\n    \"--locked\",\n    \"-p\",\n    \"desktop-core\",\n    \"--features\",\n    \"test-fixtures\",\n    \"--test\",\n    \"ui_v2_harness\"\n  ],\n  \"native_prepare\": [\n    \"cargo\",\n    \"run\",\n    \"--locked\",\n    \"-p\",\n    \"xtask\",\n    \"--\",\n    \"native-prepare\",\n    \"--chunk\",\n    \"C32\",\n    \"--target\",\n    \"aarch64-apple-darwin\",\n    \"--features\",\n    \"test-fixtures\"\n  ],\n  \"native_acceptance\": \"T09 relevant changed-UI checklist plus chunk-specific native assertions (including the UI v2 accessibility additions, DESIGN-V2 \\u00a78); real CUA/operator record required\",\n  \"live_requirement\": \"no product endpoint calls; fixture engine only\",\n  \"host_commands\": [\n    [\n      \"cargo\",\n      \"test\",\n      \"--locked\",\n      \"-p\",\n      \"desktop-core\",\n      \"--features\",\n      \"test-fixtures\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"cargo\",\n      \"test\",\n      \"--locked\",\n      \"-p\",\n      \"desktop-host\",\n      \"--features\",\n      \"test-fixtures\"\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"frontend_commands\": [\n    [\n      \"pnpm\",\n      \"--filter\",\n      \"@prisma-airs/desktop\",\n      \"exec\",\n      \"vitest\",\n      \"run\",\n      \"--config\",\n      \"vitest.config.ts\"\n    ]\n  ]\n}\nINPUT LOCK {\n  \"schema\": \"airs-v2/inputs/1\",\n  \"academy\": {\n    \"origin\": \"ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot.io/ai-security-academy.git\",\n    \"branch\": \"v2/desktop-presentation\",\n    \"base_commit\": \"7f3a28c4082a5bb278bbff5d79fab106c240155a\",\n    \"commit\": \"532809aa4bb2e1484e11ca9f7a414328260c7901\",\n    \"tree_sha256\": \"73e1c80889af89311b374808fce1f99386ced2fe95bc3a54429c40a72ce5530d\",\n    \"package_version\": \"0.176.0\"\n  }\n}\n\nC32 native [\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C32-N01\",\n    \"check\": \"The Harness renders in the v2 shell on the existing engine: the title row names the tenant, the engine and the workspace; the prerequisites checklist is hidden once satisfied; a fresh tenant shows no seeded conversation, and its first send through the composer starts a user conversation that the real engine answers, titled from that message in the rail (I12).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C32-N02\",\n    \"check\": \"A command the engine asks to run while the project is read-only is decided in the page (Approve once / Decline) after a project is chosen through the embedded project panel; while it waits, a tenant switch through the switcher, the palette and Go \\u203a Switch Tenant\\u2026 each shows the guard (Stay, Discard and switch, Archive and switch) and Stay keeps the tenant and the decision (I1, V2-N01); Decline records it and runs nothing, Approve once runs it. With changes allowed, a command is shown as May change things: Approve\\u2026 opens the native confirmation, Cancel keeps it pending and runs nothing, Allow once runs it; the guard is the one topmost modal (the rail and the page hidden beneath it) with initial focus on Stay, and Escape closes it with focus returned to the switcher.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C32-N03\",\n    \"check\": \"The workspace MCP catalog read the engine asks for (airs_desktop.mcpServers) is shown as read-only and read only once approved in the page; a change the engine proposes appears as a follow-up and writes nothing; Open the review shows it as the operator's typed review in Administration (Apply gated, still nothing written) and leaving it closes the follow-up; End conversation seals it (its transcript saved, the rail marks it ended, the engine is gone) and the next send starts a new conversation; the ended conversation is announced in a status region.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"C32-N04\",\n    \"check\": \"Keyboard: Tab from the message box reaches Send or End; at the 900\\u00d7600 minimum and 200% zoom in both themes nothing on the Harness screen lies past the window's right edge and there is no horizontal scroll bar; a focused changed control (New investigation) shows its focus ring (pixels around it change against the unfocused capture); with Reduce Motion on, the ended conversation's toast stays past 6 s until dismissed, and the system setting is restored.\"\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
